//techcrunch.com/2025/03/26/fintech-mercury-lands-300m-in-sequoia-led-series-c-doubles-valuation-to-3-5b\n- https://business.mammothtimes.com/mammothtimes/article/bizwire-2025-8-20-zed-raises-32m-series-b-led-by-sequoia-to-scale-collaborative-ai-coding-vision\n- https://siliconangle.com/2024/07/11/fireworks-ai-raises-52m-led-sequoia-522b-valuation\n- https://techcrunch.com/2024/08/29/generative-ai-coding-startup-magic-lands-320m-investment-from-eric-schmidt-atlassian-and-others/\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=579586\n- https://devops.com/streamlit-transforms-how-data-scientists-share-data-raises-35-million-in-funding-led-by-sequoia/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2023/04/28/openai-funding-valuation-chatgpt/embed/\n- https://www.maginative.com/article/thrive-capital-to-lead-purchase-of-openai-tender-offer-at-80-billion-valuation/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/01/18/sequoia-to-invest-in-anthropic-breaking-vc-taboo-on-backing-rivals-ft\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2025-05-09/sequoias-sonya-huang-on-ai-application-layer-winners-humanoid-robotics-timeline-and-enterprise-ai-battles/transcript\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-08-13/sonya-huang-on-ais-not-your-weights-not-your-product-moment-and-why-application-companies-are-building-their-own-labs/transcript\n- https://tbpn-digest.vercel.app/story/2026-01-16/sequoias-pat-grady-and-sonya-huang-declare-agi-has-arrived-and-issue-a-call-to-arms-for-founders/transcript\n- https://sequoiacap.com/podcast/david-silver-and-lasse-espeholt-of-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-age-of-experience\n- https://thenextweb.com/news/ineffable-intelligence-david-silver-sequoia-nvidia-5-billion\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/standard-intelligence-training-general-intelligence-in-pixel-space/\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/seven-questions-with-sonya-huang\n- https://sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ineffable-intelligence-a-superlearner-for-the-era-of-experience\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956\n- https://rosetta.to/u/sequoiacapital/this-is-agi-sequoia-ai-ascent-2026-keynote\n- https://sequoiacap.com/founder/david-silver\n- https://convergedigest.com/ricursive-intelligence-aims-to-accelerate-semiconductor-design/\nBackfilled 2026-11-16: known_for; seniority; check_capacity; promotion_path.\n", "person:sophia-luo": "\n# Sophia Luo\n\n## One-line take\nSophia Luo is a Greylock partner who works with early-stage AI-native application and infrastructure founders. [src](https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/)\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh: her stated mandate is AI-native applications and enabling infrastructure. [src](https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/)\n## Background\n- Luo worked as an engineer and product manager at Scale AI, then as a product engineer at Character AI; she earned three degrees from MIT. [src](https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/)\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [Greylock](https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/) | Firm profile |\n| LinkedIn | | Not found |\n| Substack / blog | [Greylock articles](https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/) | Firm profile |\n| X | | Not found |\n| GitHub | | Not found |\n| Podcasts (host) | | Not found |\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Not found in sources checked.\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n## Read these first\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n## Social engagement\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Netic | Seed | Not stated | Yes | Not stated | [Greylock](https://greylock.com/portfolio/netic/) |\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Greylock | Partner | Not stated | [Greylock](https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/) |\n| Scale AI | Former engineer and product manager | Not stated | [Greylock](https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/) |\n| Character AI | Former product engineer | Not stated | [Greylock](https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/) |\n## Open questions\n- Direct, durable headshot URL not confirmed (checked: Greylock profile).\n## Sources\n- [Greylock profile](https://greylock.com/team/sophia-luo/)\n", "person:sowmya-ranganathan": "\n# Sowmya Ranganathan\n\n## One-line take\nFormer OpenAI controller, now Lumera co-founder and CEO, with a personally attributed Campfire Series A investment. [src](https://my.financialexecutives.org/Events/Calendar-Of-Events/Meeting-Home-Page?meetingid=%7B63A08C4B-941A-F111-8341-0022480B4031%7D) [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/campfire-raises-35-million-series-a-led-by-accel-to-build-the-next-generation-ai-driven-erp-302494153.html)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh: personally named Series A investor in AI-native finance automation company Campfire. [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/campfire-raises-35-million-series-a-led-by-accel-to-build-the-next-generation-ai-driven-erp-302494153.html)\n\n## Background\nJoined OpenAI as Controller in March 2023, according to the author's own essay. The June 2025 Campfire release calls Ranganathan a former controller; exact departure date not given. [src](https://insights.lumerahq.com/p/learn-by-doing) [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/campfire-raises-35-million-series-a-led-by-accel-to-build-the-next-generation-ai-driven-erp-302494153.html)\n\nPreviously worked at Square and Rippling; Rippling identifies the former controller role. Employment dates and education were not confirmed. [src](https://www.rippling.com/en-AU/webinars/ai-accounting) [src](https://www.rippling.com/resources/ai-first-finance-tech-stack-webinar)\n\nAn April 1, 2026 FEI event identifies Ranganathan as Lumera co-founder and CEO; founding date is not stated. [src](https://my.financialexecutives.org/Events/Calendar-Of-Events/Meeting-Home-Page?meetingid=%7B63A08C4B-941A-F111-8341-0022480B4031%7D)\n\nHeadshot URL fetched successfully with HTTP 200 and an image content type on 2026-11-12. [src](https://insights.lumerahq.com/p/learn-by-doing)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [Website](https://www.lumerahq.com/) |  |\n| Substack / blog | [Substack / blog](https://insights.lumerahq.com/) |  |\n| X |  | Not found in checked sources |\n| LinkedIn |  | Not found in checked sources |\n| GitHub |  | Not found in checked sources |\n| Podcasts (host) |  | Not found in checked sources |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2025-05-05 — [Learn by Doing](https://insights.lumerahq.com/p/learn-by-doing) — Lumera Insights. Catalogued only; not mined.\n- Date not found — [Crawl, walk, run: how OpenAI automated accounting with AI](https://www.rippling.com/en-AU/webinars/ai-accounting) — Rippling. Catalogued only; not mined.\n- Date not found — [How to build a full AI-first finance tech stack](https://www.rippling.com/resources/ai-first-finance-tech-stack-webinar) — Rippling / Spendsetters. Catalogued only; not mined.\n- 2026-04-01 — [From Demo to Practice – a Hands-On Finance Session](https://my.financialexecutives.org/Events/Calendar-Of-Events/Meeting-Home-Page?meetingid=%7B63A08C4B-941A-F111-8341-0022480B4031%7D) — FEI San Francisco Bay Area. Catalogued only; not mined.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Read these first\n- 2025-05-05 — [Learn by Doing](https://insights.lumerahq.com/p/learn-by-doing) — Lumera Insights. Catalogued only; not mined.\n- Date not found — [Crawl, walk, run: how OpenAI automated accounting with AI](https://www.rippling.com/en-AU/webinars/ai-accounting) — Rippling. Catalogued only; not mined.\n- Date not found — [How to build a full AI-first finance tech stack](https://www.rippling.com/resources/ai-first-finance-tech-stack-webinar) — Rippling / Spendsetters. Catalogued only; not mined.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed in this skeleton pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Campfire | Series A | 2025-06-30 | no | Accel; Foundation Capital; Y Combinator; Capital 49; Marten Abrahamsen; Dan Kang; Alex Estevez; Michael Gordon; Jack Zhang | [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/campfire-raises-35-million-series-a-led-by-accel-to-build-the-next-generation-ai-driven-erp-302494153.html) |\n\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lumera | at: Co-founder and CEO | Not found | [src](https://my.financialexecutives.org/Events/Calendar-Of-Events/Meeting-Home-Page?meetingid=%7B63A08C4B-941A-F111-8341-0022480B4031%7D) |\n| OpenAI | at: Controller (former) | 2023-03 | [src](https://insights.lumerahq.com/p/learn-by-doing) |\n| Rippling | at: Controller (former) | Not found | [src](https://www.rippling.com/resources/ai-first-finance-tech-stack-webinar) |\n| Square | at: Finance/accounting (former) | Not found | [src](https://www.rippling.com/en-AU/webinars/ai-accounting) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Check size/capacity, personal location, education and precise prior employment dates remain unconfirmed. Seniority describes the angel-investing seat.\n- Campfire's blog displays August 27, 2025, while its company-issued financing release is dated June 30, 2025; the deal uses the original release date. [src](https://campfire.ai/blog/campfire-raises-35m-series-a-from-accel) [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/campfire-raises-35-million-series-a-led-by-accel-to-build-the-next-generation-ai-driven-erp-302494153.html)\n- Lumera's homepage was fetched but returned a JavaScript shell; role evidence comes from FEI and Rippling.\n- SEC search found no attributable filing for this person; no personal holdings or board role inferred.\n\n## Sources\n- [Source](https://campfire.ai/blog/campfire-raises-35m-series-a-from-accel)\n- [Source](https://insights.lumerahq.com/)\n- [Source](https://insights.lumerahq.com/p/learn-by-doing)\n- [Source](https://my.financialexecutives.org/Events/Calendar-Of-Events/Meeting-Home-Page?meetingid=%7B63A08C4B-941A-F111-8341-0022480B4031%7D)\n- [Source](https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/d2006741-00fa-4dfc-bc94-78aa85e97a94_3850x3850.jpeg)\n- [Source](https://www.lumerahq.com/)\n- [Source](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/campfire-raises-35-million-series-a-led-by-accel-to-build-the-next-generation-ai-driven-erp-302494153.html)\n- [Source](https://www.rippling.com/en-AU/webinars/ai-accounting)\n- [Source](https://www.rippling.com/resources/ai-first-finance-tech-stack-webinar)\n", "person:spencer-collins": "\n# Spencer Collins\n\n## One-line take\nCollins is the executive accountable for Arm's M&A and minority stakes. No AI deal release names him, so treat him as the approver rather than the sourcer.\n\n## Crux\nArm's corporate-development team \"evaluat[es] and execut[es] acquisitions and venture investments\" and manages minority stakes and board seats [src](https://careers.arm.com/job/cambridge/manager-corporate-development/33099/68013079120). It sits under Collins with legal and government affairs [src](https://www.arm.com/company/leadership). Since 2025 Arm has moved from licensing toward its own silicon, and its deals follow: AI networking (DreamBig) and inference-chip customers (Rebellions, Positron, OLIX). The constraint is SoftBank's control and Arm's preference for small deals; its CFO has said Arm historically favoured smaller acquisitions [src](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/1103594691).\n\n## Differential insight\n- Arm bought **DreamBig**, a company Samsung Catalyst had co-led at Series B (2024). That is a strategic-to-strategic exit inside this sweep's set [src](https://www.sdxcentral.com/news/arm-to-acquire-ai-networking-chipmaker-dreambig-in-265m-deal/).\n- Rebellions (Sept 2025) was reported as Arm's first startup investment in APAC [src](https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/ai-chip-startup-rebellions-valued-at-14bn-following-investment-from-arm-samsung-ventures/).\n- Below Collins, the names are dated or conflicting. Hassan Parsa was global head of corporate development 2010-2022 (aggregator) [src](https://sa.marketscreener.com/insider/HASSAN-PARSA-A0BAAH/). A now-404 SiPearl page named a \"Daniel Hunter\" as Arm's head of corporate development.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow. Arm is a strategic participant, not a lead, and no named deal person was found.\n\n## Background\nNot checked in this pass.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.arm.com/company/leadership | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- nothing found\n\n## How they think\n- nothing found in his own words\n\n## Read these first\n- Arm leadership page [src](https://www.arm.com/company/leadership)\n\n## Social engagement\nnothing found\n\n## Portfolio\nArm-level deals. Collins is not named on any of them.\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Rebellions | Series C ($250M at $1.4B) | 2025-09-30 | no | Samsung Ventures, Pegatron VC | [src](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-09-29/ai-chip-startup-rebellions-gets-funds-at-1-4-billion-valuation) |\n| DreamBig Semiconductor | Strategic (acquisition; $265M) | 2025-10 | yes | | [src](https://www.sdxcentral.com/news/arm-to-acquire-ai-networking-chipmaker-dreambig-in-265m-deal/) |\n| Positron | Series B ($230M) | 2026-02-04 | no | QIA | [src](https://news.bloomberglaw.com/private-equity/ai-chip-startup-raises-230-million-from-arm-holdings-qatar) |\n| OLIX | Series B ($312M at $3.3B) | 2026-08-03 | no | | [src](https://www.foreignpolicyjournal.com/2026/08/03/arm-holdings-nasdaq-arm-backs-312-million-funding-round-for-ai-chip-startup-olix/) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNot found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jason Child | Arm CFO; public voice on M&A appetite | | [src](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/1103594691) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is the VP of corporate development under Collins?\n- Has the DreamBig acquisition closed? MergerLinks says yes; a filing said it was expected by Q2 FY2027.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.arm.com/company/leadership\n- https://careers.arm.com/job/cambridge/manager-corporate-development/33099/68013079120\n- https://www.sdxcentral.com/news/arm-to-acquire-ai-networking-chipmaker-dreambig-in-265m-deal/\n", "person:spencer-kimball": "\n# Spencer Kimball\n\n## One-line take\nCockroach Labs co-founder and CEO with distributed-database operating experience. [src](https://www.cockroachlabs.com/author/spencer-kimball/)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium: Cockroach Labs co-founder and CEO with distributed-database operating experience. [src](https://www.cockroachlabs.com/author/spencer-kimball/)\n\n## Background\nCockroach Labs identifies Kimball as co-founder and CEO; its bio lists previous roles at Square, Viewfinder, Google, and WeGo Systems plus a UC Berkeley computer-science degree. [src](https://www.cockroachlabs.com/author/spencer-kimball/) [src](https://www.cockroachlabs.com/about/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [Source page](https://www.cockroachlabs.com/author/spencer-kimball/) | Source reviewed for this skeleton. |\n| Substack / blog |  | Not confirmed in reviewed sources. |\n| X |  | Not confirmed in reviewed sources. |\n| LinkedIn |  | Not confirmed in reviewed sources. |\n| GitHub |  | Not confirmed in reviewed sources. |\n| Podcasts (host) |  | Not confirmed in reviewed sources. |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNo source-backed appearance catalogued in this skeleton pass.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Read these first\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed in this skeleton pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\nNo source-backed personal financing attribution was established in this pass.\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cockroach Labs | at: Co-founder and CEO | 2015 | [src](https://www.cockroachlabs.com/author/spencer-kimball/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Personal check capacity, a direct durable headshot, and additional personally attributed financings were not established. (checked: [source page](https://www.cockroachlabs.com/author/spencer-kimball/); [additional source](https://www.cockroachlabs.com/about/))\n- Prior-employment dates and promotion chronology remain incomplete where the reviewed sources do not date them. (checked: [source page](https://www.cockroachlabs.com/author/spencer-kimball/))\n\n## Sources\n- [Source 1](https://www.cockroachlabs.com/author/spencer-kimball/)\n- [Source 2](https://www.cockroachlabs.com/about/)\n", "person:spencer-powers": "\n# Spencer Powers\n\n## One-line take\nDRW's Head of Special Investments [src](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo). He co-founded the photonic-chip company Lightelligence and holds board and officer seats at DRW's compute company, Silicon Data. For photonics or compute-market founders he is the most direct person at DRW.\n\n## Crux\n- **What makes him win:** he is a hardware co-founder (Lightelligence, MIT optical AI chips) now sitting in the seat of a proprietary trading firm that invests its own balance sheet. DRW has no outside LPs (see [firms/drw-venture-capital.md](../firms/drw-venture-capital.md)).\n- **How the seat works:** it is a control and governance seat, not a VC check-writer's.\n  - He chairs Plainsight's board and speaks for it when the CEO changes [src](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-announces-jonathan-simkins-as-new-ceo-succeeding-kit-merker).\n  - He has been a director of Silicon Data's legal entity since the seed. He is also an executive officer there [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml).\n- **Constraint:** he works inside DRW's structure, where Don Wilson co-sits on the Silicon Data board. Final approval likely rests with Wilson (inference, unconfirmed).\n- **What he'd never do (inference):** probably not a passive minority co-investor in a crowded frontier-lab seed. His named deals are DRW-incubated or DRW-controlled companies.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **On Silicon Data's board from the seed, not just the A.** The March 2025 seed Form D for Silicon Derivatives Inc. ($4,724,994 sold, 12 investors) lists him as a director at DRW's address: \"Spencer Powers 540 W. Madison, Suite 2500 Chicago\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml).\n  - By the August 2026 A ($30,499,940, 16 investors, Valor Atreides lead) he is \"Executive Officer\" and director [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml).\n  - This corrects an earlier repo row that dated the seat to 2026-07.\n  - The $4.72M seed figure matches Silicon Data's announced $4.7M seed. That strengthens the Silicon Derivatives = Silicon Data match, which is still an inference.\n- **Plainsight looks DRW-owned.** Plainsight Technologies Inc. was incorporated in 2024. Its Form D shows $12M sold to **one** investor, with Powers as sole director at DRW's Chicago address [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2027185/000202718524000001/primary_doc.xml).\n  - He is \"Chairman of the Board at Plainsight Technologies, and Head of Special Investments\" [src](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo).\n  - Reading: a DRW recapitalization or full ownership of the ex-Sixgill computer-vision business. The single investor is not named, so this is unconfirmed.\n- **A shared back office.** Sure People's Form D (HR analytics; $5M from one investor; Powers a director) lists phone \"3128364073\" [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2046502/000204650224000001/primary_doc.xml). Silicon Derivatives' Form Ds list the same number.\n  - One office, likely DRW special investments (unconfirmed), handles filings for both. Other single-investor holdings under the same desk may exist.\n- **He runs CEO succession.** On replacing Plainsight's CEO in December 2025 he said: \"Kit accomplished what the company needed during a pivotal time for Plainsight\" [src](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-announces-jonathan-simkins-as-new-ceo-succeeding-kit-merker).\n- **How he got the chip background:** \"I was an MBA student at MIT in 2015 when I met Yichen\" [src](https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/). Yichen Shen is Lightelligence's founder-CEO.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.**\n- **For:** he is a real, named decision-maker at a balance-sheet investor that co-led Silicon Data's seed and has backed GPU supply (Crusoe, Vast.ai per the firm file). Photonics and compute-market founders have a natural ally in him. DRW's money has no LP ownership constraints, which suits the owner's dislike of rigid-ownership LPs.\n- **Against:**\n  - None of his named deals is a frontier-lab or a $20M-style seed.\n  - His seat is closer to an owner or operator than a co-investor. The 2025 seed was $4.7M, Plainsight $12M from one investor, Sure People $5M.\n  - Seniority maps loosely: \"Head of\" at a trading firm is set here to `partner`, which is an approximation.\n- **Deals that matter:** Silicon Data Seed (2025) and Series A (2026).\n\n## Background\n- BA in Economics, Boston College (per search snippets of LinkedIn/TheOrg; unconfirmed).\n- Roles at IDEO, Mercer, Liberty Mutual and Ropes & Gray appear as logos on TheOrg [src](https://theorg.com/org/drw/org-chart/spencer-powers). Aggregator; titles and dates unknown.\n- Co-founded ArtLifting (benefit corporation for artists) in 2013. Director 2013 to 2022-03 per MarketScreener [src](https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/SPENCER-POWERS-A1WWYO/).\n- MIT Sloan MBA '16 [src](https://physics.mit.edu/news/accelerating-ai-at-the-speed-of-light).\n- Co-founded Lux Labs, an MIT spinout per a TheOrg search snippet. Unconfirmed, and not to be confused with Lux Capital's Lux Labs.\n- Co-founded Lightelligence (founded 2017) with Yichen Shen and Marin Soljačić. Sources call him a co-founder and board member [src](https://physics.mit.edu/news/accelerating-ai-at-the-speed-of-light), [src](https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/). Title \"President\" and tenure 2018-04 to 2023-08 come from TheOrg via search snippet (aggregator, unconfirmed).\n- Moved to DRW as Head of Special Investments. Start 2023-08 per TheOrg (aggregator). Confirmed in the title by Plainsight's 2024-01-24 release.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found | No DRW team page names him (drw.com/global-investments checked 2026-11-11) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/scp1 | Surfaced in search for \"Spencer Powers Lightelligence\"; identity match unconfirmed (one snippet showed a Seattle profile) |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n| Aggregator | https://theorg.com/org/drw/org-chart/spencer-powers | TheOrg, unverified profile, no photo |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2022-04 — Physics World Optics and Photonics Briefing — Lightelligence interview (co-founder voice on Lightelligence's origin) — https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/\n- No DRW-era talks or podcasts found.\n\n## How they think\n- Frames enterprise AI as an adoption problem: Plainsight serves companies \"underserved because of the complexity of consuming AI\" [src](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo).\n- Hands-on governance: picks and replaces CEOs at companies he chairs (Merker 2024, Simkins 2025).\n- No public contrarian views found.\n\n## Read these first\n- Silicon Derivatives Form Ds, [2025](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml) and [2026](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml): his seat on Silicon Data from the seed onward.\n- [Plainsight 2024 CEO release](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo): his only substantive on-record quotes.\n- [Physics World 2022](https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/): Lightelligence origin.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: no public X, Substack or podcast presence.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Silicon Data (Silicon Derivatives Inc.) | Seed ($4.7M); director | 2025-03-20 | DRW co-led with Jump | Jump Trading Group; Woodside AI; Wintermute Ventures; Sancus; SoGal | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Silicon Data | Series A ($30.5M); re-up, executive officer and director | 2026-08 (first sale 2026-07-17) | no (Valor Atreides AI Fund led) | CME Group; F-Prime; Samsung; VanEck | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Plainsight | Undisclosed ($12M, single investor); sole director, chairman | 2024-05-31 | unknown (investor unnamed) | none listed | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2027185/000202718524000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Sure People (not AI-core) | Undisclosed ($5M, single investor); director | 2024-11-08 | unknown | none listed | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2046502/000204650224000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Silicon Data:**\n  - DRW incubated it, per the firm file. Powers and Don Wilson were both directors from the March 2025 seed.\n  - Valor's Vivek Pattipati joined the board with the 2026 A, and Powers added an officer role.\n  - First in: yes (seed). Re-up: yes (A).\n  - Intro path and pricing role not disclosed.\n- **Plainsight:**\n  - Plainsight is the former Sixgill. Aggregators list DRW Venture Capital as an earlier investor (unconfirmed).\n  - The 2024 restructuring into Plainsight Technologies Inc. had a single $12M investor and Powers as sole director. He chaired the board through two CEO changes.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DRW | Head of Special Investments | 2023-08 (aggregator) / by 2024-01 | [Plainsight](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo) |\n| Don Wilson | Co-director, Silicon Derivatives | 2025-03 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Rongnan Li | CEO of Silicon Derivatives; co-director | 2025-03 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Vivek Pattipati (Valor) | Co-director | 2026 | [Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Yichen Shen, Marin Soljačić | Lightelligence co-founders | 2015 (met) / 2017 | [Physics World](https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/) |\n| Kit Merker, Jonathan Simkins | Plainsight CEOs hired under his chairmanship | 2024, 2025 | [2024](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo), [2025](https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-announces-jonathan-simkins-as-new-ceo-succeeding-kit-merker) |\n\n**How a founder or co-investor reaches him:** no public channel was found.\n- **Best route:** through Silicon Data (CEO Rongnan Li, who may be Carmen Li; not confirmed) or Valor's Vivek Pattipati, both co-directors.\n- **For photonics founders:** the MIT and Lightelligence network (Yichen Shen).\n- **Mail:** the DRW address, 540 W. Madison, Suite 2500, Chicago.\n\n## Open questions\n- Is Rongnan Li the same person as Silicon Data's CEO Carmen Li? No source links the names.\n- Who is the single investor in Plainsight ($12M) and Sure People ($5M)? Is DRW behind Sure People?\n- Does Special Investments sit under DRW Global Investments, which describes \"longer-term, strategic investments\" [src](https://www.drw.com/global-investments)? Unconfirmed.\n- Does he still hold Lightelligence equity or a board seat? MarketScreener shows no end date. If so, that is a conflict and also a lens: DRW and photonics.\n- Who approves his checks? Likely Don Wilson (inference).\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117125000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2061171/000206117126000003/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2027185/000202718524000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2046502/000204650224000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-technologies-names-kit-merker-as-ceo\n- https://go.plainsight.ai/blog/plainsight-announces-jonathan-simkins-as-new-ceo-succeeding-kit-merker\n- https://physicsworld.com/a/optical-chipmaker-focuses-on-high-performance-computing/\n- https://physics.mit.edu/news/accelerating-ai-at-the-speed-of-light\n- https://theorg.com/org/drw/org-chart/spencer-powers (aggregator)\n- https://www.marketscreener.com/insider/SPENCER-POWERS-A1WWYO/ (aggregator)\n- https://www.drw.com/global-investments\nBackfilled 2026-11-16: photo\n", "person:sri-viswanath": "\n# Sri Viswanath\n\n*Written 2026-11-11 (queue-31, owner pick). The queue brief called him a \"Tidal Partners founder\". That is not supported. Tidal Partners is a strategic M&A advisory firm co-founded by David Handler and David Neequaye, and he is not on its team page ([tidalpartners.com](https://www.tidalpartners.com/)). Every primary source puts him at Sun/VMware, then CTO at Groupon and Atlassian, then a Coatue GP, then founder of Sycamore. The repo's \"ex-Tidal\" tags belong to other people (Lina Zhuo, Anavi Tekriwal), which is probably where the mix-up came from.*\n\n## One-line take\nHe did nearly all of Coatue's first-cheque AI work from 2022 to 2025. He is now a founder whose cap table maps who trusts him, and his ex-portfolio founders are on it.\n\n## Crux\n- **What made him win:** he came in as a big-company CTO who had run a cloud platform migration, so his edge was platform theses: an open-source model lab (Stability), a robot foundation model (Skild) and compliance infrastructure (Norm). He met founders before they had companies, as with Norm and WisdomAI ([Norm](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-partnership-with-norm-ai), [WisdomAI](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/wisdomai-the-future-of-data-insights)).\n- **What constrains him now:** he no longer writes institutional cheques. His leverage is reference value and angel-network routing, not capital ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/30/former-coatue-partner-raises-huge-65m-seed-for-enterprise-ai-agent-startup/)).\n- **What he'd never do (by record):** back an app layer without a platform beneath it. His stated rule is platform before product ([Sycamore blog](https://sycamore.so/blog/company/building-the-ai-native-enterprise-os/)).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His Coatue portfolio founders backed his startup.** Sycamore's angels include John Nay (Norm Ai), Soham Mazumdar (WisdomAI), and Deepak Pathak and Abhinav Gupta (Skild AI), all companies he led or co-led at Coatue ([release](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed)). He says \"The round came together through long-standing relationships.\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/30/former-coatue-partner-raises-huge-65m-seed-for-enterprise-ai-agent-startup/), 2026-03-30)\n- **His investing seat was market research for Sycamore.** \"At Coatue, I saw enterprise after enterprise spinning up agent proofs-of-concept\" ([Sycamore blog](https://sycamore.so/blog/company/building-the-ai-native-enterprise-os/)). His former employer then co-led the seed, and co-founder Thomas Laffont is quoted in the release ([release](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed)).\n- **Platform-first rule from Atlassian:** \"before you can scale any product, you have to build the platform beneath it\" ([Sycamore blog](https://sycamore.so/blog/company/building-the-ai-native-enterprise-os/)).\n- **He bet on a slow robotics rollout while co-leading the biggest robotics A of 2024.** He co-wrote \"We believe robotics cannot have a ChatGPT Moment\" ([Coatue, 2024-07-17](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/robotics-wont-have-a-chatgpt-moment)) eight days after co-leading Skild's $300M Series A ([Skild](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a), 2024-07-09).\n- **Defense side-thesis:** co-wrote Coatue's industrial-base memo with Armin Kohan, which says \"wars are no longer won solely through superior spending\" ([Coatue, 2025-02-26](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/americas-industrial-reboot-a-massive-tech-opportunity)). No defense deal by him was found.\n- **His headcount claims differ by source.** He told TechCrunch he \"scaled the engineering org to 7,000+\" at Atlassian ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/30/former-coatue-partner-raises-huge-65m-seed-for-enterprise-ai-agent-startup/)). A fetch summary of his own blog gave \"800 to nearly 4,000\", which could not be checked in the raw HTML (hypothesis).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** He is no longer a cheque-writer, so he isn't an LP-fit or co-investor target, and seniority is set to `angel` for that reason. He still matters three ways:\n1. As a reference on Coatue's early-AI book: [Stability AI](https://stability.ai/news/stability-ai-announces-101-million-in-funding-for-open-source-artificial-intelligence) first round, Skild A, Norm seed/A/B, WisdomAI seed and Legion A.\n2. As a node linking Coatue, Lightspeed (Raviraj Jain), Skild, Ndea (Chollet, Knoop), OpenAI alumni (McGrew, Tworek, Katti) and Atlassian alumni (Jay Simons at BOND, Anu Bharadwaj).\n3. As a test case of the investor-turned-founder seed: a $65M seed with a growth fund as co-lead.\n\n## Background\n- Sun Microsystems and VMware, then CTO of Groupon, then CTO of Atlassian, where he led the cloud transformation ([release](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed)). Exact dates for each role were not found in primary sources.\n- 2022-06-20: Coatue announces him as a new partner investing in AI ([Coatue/Forbes](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/coatue-adds-atlassian-cto-as-new-partner-investing-in-ai)).\n- Fall 2025: left the full-time VC role ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/30/former-coatue-partner-raises-huge-65m-seed-for-enterprise-ai-agent-startup/)). His last bylined Coatue deal is Legion, 2025-07-30 ([Coatue](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai)).\n- 2026-03-30: Sycamore announces a $65M seed with him as founder and CEO ([release](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed)).\n- Education: not found.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://sycamore.so | Company site; founder blog post (2026-03) |\n| Substack / blog | https://sycamore.so/blog/company/building-the-ai-native-enterprise-os/ | One founding essay |\n| X | not found | Not checked (web search budget exhausted) |\n| LinkedIn | not found | Not checked |\n| GitHub | not found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-03 — Lightspeed \"The Investment Memo\" — How AI Can Make Every Employee a Builder — [YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmiwMUa_wpA). Raviraj Jain says on it that he has known him for years as a fellow investor. The auto-transcript has no speaker labels, so this was not mined for his own quotes (see `raviraj-jain`).\n- 2022-06 — Forbes — hire coverage ([Coatue press page](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/coatue-adds-atlassian-cto-as-new-partner-investing-in-ai)). Forbes returned 403, so the text wasn't read.\n\n## How they think\n- **Platform before product**, from the Atlassian cloud migration ([blog](https://sycamore.so/blog/company/building-the-ai-native-enterprise-os/)).\n- **Foundation-model layers in new domains:** open-source image models (Stability, 2022) and robot foundation models (Skild, 2024) ([Skild](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a)).\n- **Physical AI rolls out slowly**, without a single ChatGPT moment ([memo](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/robotics-wont-have-a-chatgpt-moment)).\n- **Calls so far:** Skild was re-upped by Coatue at the Series C (>$14B, 2026-01) and Norm became a unicorn ($1.2B, 2026-07); both are in `coatue-portfolio-deals.csv`. Stability is the weaker outcome: a later leadership crisis, details not covered here.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Building the AI-native enterprise OS](https://sycamore.so/blog/company/building-the-ai-native-enterprise-os/): why he left investing.\n2. [Robotics won't have a ChatGPT moment](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/robotics-wont-have-a-chatgpt-moment): his physical-AI frame.\n3. [Our partnership with Norm Ai](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-partnership-with-norm-ai): the pre-founding access playbook.\n4. [America's Industrial Reboot](https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/americas-industrial-reboot-a-massive-tech-opportunity): defense and industrial thesis.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. X and LinkedIn were not checked because the web search budget was exhausted.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Stability AI | Seed ($101M, first institutional) | 2022-10-17 | co-led (Coatue) | Lightspeed, O'Shaughnessy Ventures | [release](https://stability.ai/news/stability-ai-announces-101-million-in-funding-for-open-source-artificial-intelligence) |\n| Norm Ai | Seed | 2023 | yes (Coatue) | | [Coatue](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-partnership-with-norm-ai) |\n| Norm Ai | Series A ($27M), board seat | 2024-06-25 | yes (Coatue) | Blackstone, Citi, New York Life, TIAA, Bain Capital Ventures | [PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/norm-ai-raises-27-million-series-a-to-expand-ai-driven-regulatory-compliance-platform-302181545.html) |\n| Skild AI | Series A ($300M at $1.5B) | 2024-07-09 | co-led (Coatue) | Lightspeed, SoftBank, Bezos Expeditions; Felicis, Sequoia, Menlo, General Catalyst, CRV, SV Angel | [Skild](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a) |\n| Norm Ai | Series B (third event) | 2025-03-11 | yes (Coatue) | Craft, Vanguard, Blackstone, Bain Capital, NY Life, Citi, TIAA (cumulative) | [Coatue](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-partnership-with-norm-ai) |\n| WisdomAI | Seed ($23M) | 2025-05-07 | yes (Coatue) | not named | [Coatue](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/wisdomai-the-future-of-data-insights) |\n| Legion Security | Series A | 2025-07-30 | yes (Coatue) | Accel, Picture Capital | [Coatue](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai) |\n| Magical | Undisclosed (personal angel) | not disclosed | no | Andy Chen, Jay Simons, Dylan Field and others | [Magical](https://www.getmagical.com/about-us) |\n\nThe Coatue-era rows live in `data/rows/coatue-portfolio-deals.csv` and are not repeated in this page's row file. The Sycamore cap table (Coatue, Lightspeed, Abstract, Dell Technologies Capital, 8VC, Fellows Fund, E14 Fund and 18 named angels) is in `data/rows/roundmap-sycamore-rounds.csv`.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Norm Ai:** Coatue met John Nay months before he founded Norm, invested around founding in summer 2023 and led the next two rounds. He took the board seat at the A ([Coatue](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-partnership-with-norm-ai), [PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/norm-ai-raises-27-million-series-a-to-expand-ai-driven-regulatory-compliance-platform-302181545.html)). Nay later angel-invested in Sycamore.\n- **WisdomAI:** \"We first met the team nearly a year before they founded WisdomAI.\" ([Coatue](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/wisdomai-the-future-of-data-insights)). Founder Soham Mazumdar later angel-invested in Sycamore.\n- **Sycamore (his own):** a $65M seed co-led by Coatue and Lightspeed. On the Coatue side Laffont is quoted. On the Lightspeed side is Raviraj Jain, his fellow Skild A co-lead ([release](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed)). Dell Technologies Capital says Fortune 500 companies were building on it months after founding ([DTC](https://www.delltechnologiescapital.com/resources/sycamore-building-the-agent-os-for-the-future)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Coatue | General Partner (former) | 2022-06 to 2025 | [hire](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/coatue-adds-atlassian-cto-as-new-partner-investing-in-ai) |\n| Thomas Laffont | Former boss; co-led his seed | 2022 | [release](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed) |\n| Raviraj Jain (Lightspeed) | Skild co-lead; Sycamore co-lead | 2024 | [Skild](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a) |\n| Vibhor Khanna, Abhi Srinivas, Nina Gerson, Armin Kohan | Coatue co-authors and juniors | 2024 to 2025 | [WisdomAI](https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/wisdomai-the-future-of-data-insights) |\n| John Nay, Soham Mazumdar, Deepak Pathak, Abhinav Gupta | Portfolio founders turned his angels | 2023 to 2026 | [release](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed) |\n| Jay Simons (BOND), Anu Bharadwaj | Atlassian alumni; angels in Sycamore | | [release](https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Atlassian and Groupon tenure dates. The Atlassian blog URLs returned 404 and Forbes returned 403.\n- Whether he kept the Norm Ai board seat after leaving Coatue.\n- Whether Coatue still holds his other early-AI relationships, and who now covers them (Frank Long? Vibhor Khanna?).\n- His X and LinkedIn presence and any podcasts beyond Lightspeed's (web search was unavailable this pass).\n- Tidal Partners: the queue brief's attribution was checked against [tidalpartners.com](https://www.tidalpartners.com/) and doesn't hold. If the owner meant another person who founded Tidal, that person still needs identifying.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/coatue-adds-atlassian-cto-as-new-partner-investing-in-ai\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/30/former-coatue-partner-raises-huge-65m-seed-for-enterprise-ai-agent-startup/\n- https://sycamore.so/press-releases/sycamore-raises-65m-seed\n- https://sycamore.so/blog/company/building-the-ai-native-enterprise-os/\n- https://www.delltechnologiescapital.com/resources/sycamore-building-the-agent-os-for-the-future\n- https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/our-partnership-with-norm-ai\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/wisdomai-the-future-of-data-insights\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/press/legion-scaling-security-with-ai\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/robotics-wont-have-a-chatgpt-moment\n- https://www.coatue.com/blog/perspective/americas-industrial-reboot-a-massive-tech-opportunity\n- https://stability.ai/news/stability-ai-announces-101-million-in-funding-for-open-source-artificial-intelligence\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/norm-ai-raises-27-million-series-a-to-expand-ai-driven-regulatory-compliance-platform-302181545.html\n- https://www.getmagical.com/about-us\n- https://www.tidalpartners.com/\n", "person:sridhar-ramaswamy-gv": "\n# Sridhar Ramaswamy\n\n## One-line take\nSnowflake CEO and Neeva co-founder. [src](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/authors/sridhar-ramaswamy/)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh (research classification): named early backer of Resolve AI, relevant to the AI-native automation lens. [src](https://website-prod.resolve.ai/blog/introducing-resolve-ai)\n\n## Background\nSnowflake's current author bio identifies Ramaswamy as CEO, Neeva co-founder, former Google Ads & Commerce SVP and a Brown computer-science Ph.D. [src](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/authors/sridhar-ramaswamy/)\n\nJoined Snowflake with the May 2023 Neeva acquisition after co-founding Neeva in 2019. Previously held research roles at Bell Labs, Lucent Technologies and Bellcore and was a Greylock venture partner from October 2018; precise end dates are not supplied here. [src](https://www.snowflake.com/en/podcast/sridhar-ramaswamy/)\n\nSEC-filed offer letter makes the CEO transition effective 2024-02-27 and locates the role in San Mateo. [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1640147/000164014724000033/exhibit101-confirmatoryoff.htm)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [link](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/authors/sridhar-ramaswamy/) |  |\n| Substack / blog | [link](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/authors/sridhar-ramaswamy/) |  |\n| X |  | Not found in this pass |\n| LinkedIn | [link](https://www.linkedin.com/in/sridhar-ramaswamy/) |  |\n| GitHub |  | Not found in this pass |\n| Podcasts (host) |  | Not found in this pass |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-05-14 — The Data Cloud Podcast / Steve Hamm — [Unlocking Enterprise AI with Sridhar Ramaswamy, CEO at Snowflake](https://www.snowflake.com/en/podcast/sridhar-ramaswamy/) — catalogue only; not mined.\n- 2026-08-18 — Snowflake — [Intelligence Efficiency: More Model Choice, Smarter Routing](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/ai-intelligence-efficiency-dynamic-model-routing/) — catalogue only; not mined.\n- 2026-06-17 — Snowflake — [Powering the Agentic Enterprise: Turning Enterprise Context into Governed Agentic Action](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/agentic-enterprise-snowflake-accenture/) — catalogue only; not mined.\n- 2026-03-18 — Snowflake — [Powering the Era of the Agentic Enterprise](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/agentic-enterprise-control-plane/) — catalogue only; not mined.\n- 2025-10-21 — Snowflake — [When Operations Fail, Your Data Platform Can’t: Why Snowflake Disaster Recovery Is a Must Have](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/snowflake-data-platform/) — catalogue only; not mined.\n- 2023-11-27 — Snowflake — [Predictions for the Dawning AI Age: What to Expect in 2024 and Beyond](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/predictions-ai-age-2024/) — catalogue only; not mined.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Read these first\nNot ranked yet (skeleton page); media catalogue above is for the insight lane.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot researched in this skeleton pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Resolve AI | Seed | 2024-09-24 | No | Greylock; Unusual Ventures | [src](https://website-prod.resolve.ai/blog/introducing-resolve-ai) |\n\nCompany launch page names the person. Date is displayed publication date, not a verified closing date; batch says October 2024. [src](https://website-prod.resolve.ai/blog/introducing-resolve-ai)\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Snowflake | CEO | 2024-02-27 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1640147/000164014724000033/exhibit101-confirmatoryoff.htm) |\n| Snowflake | former SVP, AI | 2023-05 | [src](https://www.snowflake.com/en/podcast/sridhar-ramaswamy/) |\n| Neeva | co-founder | 2019 | [src](https://www.snowflake.com/en/podcast/sridhar-ramaswamy/) |\n| Google | former SVP, Ads and Commerce | not confirmed | [src](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/authors/sridhar-ramaswamy/) |\n| Greylock | former Venture Partner | 2018-10 | [src](https://www.snowflake.com/en/podcast/sridhar-ramaswamy/) |\n| Bell Labs | former researcher | not confirmed | [src](https://www.snowflake.com/en/podcast/sridhar-ramaswamy/) |\n| Lucent Technologies | former researcher | not confirmed | [src](https://www.snowflake.com/en/podcast/sridhar-ramaswamy/) |\n| Bellcore | former researcher | not confirmed | [src](https://www.snowflake.com/en/podcast/sridhar-ramaswamy/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- The batch/registry slug ends in -gv, but the verified venture affiliation is Greylock, not GV. Kept the assigned canonical ID; no second Sridhar page or GV employment row created.\n- Personal check capacity, early research employment dates, exact Greylock departure and graduation date remain unconfirmed.\n- The full SEC proxy PDF could not be retrieved (size limit / direct 403); used the accessible SEC CEO offer letter instead.\n- Resolve AI: fetched company launch is dated 2024-09-24, whereas batch rank_reason says 2024-10. Used the displayed source date with an explicit caveat; actual close date remains unconfirmed. [src](https://website-prod.resolve.ai/blog/introducing-resolve-ai)\n- `check_capacity` and `check_size` are blank: no fetched source establishes personal check limits. `fast_promotion: unknown` avoids inferring an investing promotion from an operating-company title.\n- Headshot was extracted from the linked firm/personal page and returned HTTP 200 with image content type on 2026-11-12. [src](https://www.snowflake.com/en/podcast/sridhar-ramaswamy/)\n\n## Sources\n- [Source 1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1640147/000164014724000033/exhibit101-confirmatoryoff.htm)\n- [Source 2](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/authors/sridhar-ramaswamy/)\n- [Source 3](https://website-prod.resolve.ai/blog/introducing-resolve-ai)\n- [Source 4](https://www.snowflake.com/en/podcast/sridhar-ramaswamy/)\n- [Source 5](https://www.linkedin.com/in/sridhar-ramaswamy/)\n- [Source 6](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/ai-intelligence-efficiency-dynamic-model-routing/)\n- [Source 7](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/agentic-enterprise-snowflake-accenture/)\n- [Source 8](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/agentic-enterprise-control-plane/)\n- [Source 9](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/snowflake-data-platform/)\n- [Source 10](https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/predictions-ai-age-2024/)\n", "person:srini-ananth": "\n# Srini Ananth\n\n## One-line take\nHe has the best AI-infrastructure record at Intel Capital: he led Astera Labs from its Series A to the 2024 IPO, and Ayar Labs, Eliyan and Mueon are on his board list ([bio](https://www.intelcapital.com/team/srinivas-ananth)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins.** He is an engineer (MSEE) with a decade of semiconductor operating work. He finds interconnect, photonics and power companies before they are obvious: Astera's A in 2018 at a ~$19M post (Wikipedia, secondary; see deal row).\n- **Constraint.** Intel announced in January 2025 that Intel Capital would become a standalone fund, then reversed course on its 2025-04-24 Q1 call: the unit stays inside Intel, monetizes the existing portfolio and is \"more selective on new investments\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/24/intel-reverses-course-opts-not-to-spin-out-intel-capital); [GCV](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/corporate-venturer/intel-no-longer-intends-to-spin-out-its-cvc-arm/?format=md)).\n- **Would never.** nothing sourced.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **EPIC thesis.** \"What convinced us to back EPIC was not just the technology\" ([Intel Capital](https://www.intelcapital.com/powering-the-next-generation-of-ai-compute-epic-microsystems/)); the post is co-written with Kike Miralles.\n- **Kepler Computing** is on his current-investments list. Kepler came out of stealth on 2026-09-10 with $468M from GlobalFoundries, Intel Capital, AMD Ventures and Bill Gates, which included a $245M CHIPS Act award ([AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/ferroelectric-memory-startup-kepler-computing-exits-stealth-with-468m-to)). Intel's round is not named.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh: Astera Labs, Ayar Labs (A to D), Eliyan, Inworld AI (co-led A), Mueon (led seed), Salience Labs, EPIC Microsystems, Upscale AI, FieldAI, Kepler Computing.\n\n## Background\n- MBA, UCLA; MSEE, Arizona State ([bio](https://www.intelcapital.com/team/srinivas-ananth)).\n- Over a decade of operating work in engineering, BD and product (IoT, mobile, semiconductors); the bio does not name employers ([bio](https://www.intelcapital.com/team/srinivas-ananth)).\n- Joined Intel Capital in 2015; now Managing Director. Board director or observer at Astera Labs, Ayar Labs, Common Sense Machines, Eliyan, Inworld AI, SiPhox and VeriSIM Life; former observer at BabbleLabs and Joby ([bio](https://www.intelcapital.com/team/srinivas-ananth)).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.intelcapital.com/team/srinivas-ananth | Intel Capital bio, fetched 2026-11-11 |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.intelcapital.com/news | Investment posts (Salience, EPIC, Mueon, FieldAI) |\n| X | not found |  |\n| LinkedIn | not checked |  |\n| GitHub | n/a |  |\n| Podcasts (host) | none |  |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nnothing found\n\n## How they think\n- AI data-center bottlenecks below the GPU: chiplet interconnect, optical I/O, power delivery, memory.\n\n## Read these first\n- [EPIC Microsystems post](https://www.intelcapital.com/powering-the-next-generation-of-ai-compute-epic-microsystems/).\n- [Salience Labs post](https://www.intelcapital.com/salience-labs-secures-125-million-in-series-b-funding-to-scale-photonic-networking-chips-for-ai-datacenter-infrastructure/).\n- [Astera Labs IPO post](https://www.intelcapital.com/team/srinivas-ananth) (listed 2024-03-22).\n\n## Social engagement\nnothing found: no active public X/Substack presence located (2026-11-11).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Astera Labs | Series A -> D | 2018-03 to 2022-05 | no (led Intel's entry) | Sutter Hill; Fidelity | [bio](https://www.intelcapital.com/team/srinivas-ananth) |\n| Ayar Labs | Series A -> D | 2018-11 to 2024-12 | no | Playground; Founders Fund | [bio](https://www.intelcapital.com/team/srinivas-ananth) |\n| Eliyan | Series A $40M | 2022-11 | no | Tracker Capital | [siliconangle](https://siliconangle.com/?p=597893) |\n| Inworld AI | Series A $50M | 2022-08 | co-led | Section 32 | [Intel Capital](https://www.intelcapital.com/pushing-the-boundaries-of-immersive-characters-our-investment-in-inworld-ai/) |\n| Mueon | Seed $15.5M / Series A $65M | 2025-09 / 2026-10 | led seed | Maverick Silicon | [Intel Capital](https://www.intelcapital.com/reimagining-data-center-infrastructure-for-the-ai-era-intel-capital-leads-mueons-seed-round/) |\n| EPIC Microsystems | Series A $21M | 2026-03-25 | no | Seligman Ventures | [Intel Capital](https://www.intelcapital.com/powering-the-next-generation-of-ai-compute-epic-microsystems/) |\n| Salience Labs | Series B $125M | 2026-10-14 | no | Bullhound | [Intel Capital](https://www.intelcapital.com/salience-labs-secures-125-million-in-series-b-funding-to-scale-photonic-networking-chips-for-ai-datacenter-infrastructure/) |\n| Kepler Computing | Undisclosed (part of $468M disclosed) | 2026-09-10 | no | GlobalFoundries; AMD Ventures; Gates | [AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/ferroelectric-memory-startup-kepler-computing-exits-stealth-with-468m-to) |\n| Upscale AI | Series A $200M | 2026-01-21 | no | Tiger Global | data rows |\n\n## Deal stories\nnothing found\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kike Miralles | Co-author on EPIC post | 2026-03 | [Intel Capital](https://www.intelcapital.com/powering-the-next-generation-of-ai-compute-epic-microsystems/) |\n| Anthony Lin | Head of Intel Capital; both list Upscale AI and Mueon |  | [bio](https://www.intelcapital.com/team/anthony-lin) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Which Kepler round Intel joined, and who holds the seat.\n- His pre-2015 employers.\n- The three stealth companies his bio mentions.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.intelcapital.com/team/srinivas-ananth\n- https://www.intelcapital.com/powering-the-next-generation-of-ai-compute-epic-microsystems/\n- https://aiweekly.co/alerts/ferroelectric-memory-startup-kepler-computing-exits-stealth-with-468m-to\n- https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/24/intel-reverses-course-opts-not-to-spin-out-intel-capital\n", "person:srinivas-narayanan": "\n# Srinivas Narayanan\n\n## One-line take\nFormer OpenAI CTO of B2B Applications and former Meta engineering leader. [src](https://blog.southparkcommons.com/p/on-building-in-uncertainty-and-future)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh (research classification): named early backer of Resolve AI, relevant to the AI-native automation lens. [src](https://website-prod.resolve.ai/blog/introducing-resolve-ai)\n\n## Background\nOwn post dated 2025-09-02 announces the transition from leading Applied Engineering to CTO of B2B Applications. [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/srinivasnarayanan_im-excited-to-share-that-im-transitioning-activity-7368705215933374466-frjZ)\n\nOwn departure post dated 2026-04-17 says he will leave OpenAI at the end of the following week after three years; no next employer is named. Dates are from the public page's datePublished metadata. [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/srinivasnarayanan_after-3-incredible-years-i-am-leaving-openai-activity-7451025653967114240-NW6Y)\n\nIndiaspora's member bio records earlier Meta AI engineering leadership, founding roles at Viralizr and Tavant, and IBM Research Almaden. It still labels OpenAI as current; the departure post supersedes that portion. [src](https://indiaspora.org/members/srinivas-narayanan/)\n\nEducation and graduation dates: not confirmed in the fetched first-person and employer sources.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [link](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/srinivasnarayanan_after-3-incredible-years-i-am-leaving-openai-activity-7451025653967114240-NW6Y) |  |\n| Substack / blog |  | Not found in this pass |\n| X |  | Not found in this pass |\n| LinkedIn | [link](https://www.linkedin.com/in/srinivasnarayanan/) |  |\n| GitHub |  | Not found in this pass |\n| Podcasts (host) |  | Not found in this pass |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-09-18 — South Park Commons / Prateek Mehta — [On Building in Uncertainty, and Future of AI Safety](https://blog.southparkcommons.com/p/on-building-in-uncertainty-and-future) — catalogue only; not mined.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Read these first\nNot ranked yet (skeleton page); media catalogue above is for the insight lane.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot researched in this skeleton pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Resolve AI | Seed | 2024-09-24 | No | Greylock; Unusual Ventures | [src](https://website-prod.resolve.ai/blog/introducing-resolve-ai) |\n\nCompany launch page names the person. Date is displayed publication date, not a verified closing date; batch says October 2024. [src](https://website-prod.resolve.ai/blog/introducing-resolve-ai)\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenAI | former engineering leader; CTO of B2B Applications | not confirmed | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/srinivasnarayanan_after-3-incredible-years-i-am-leaving-openai-activity-7451025653967114240-NW6Y) |\n| Meta | former VP of Engineering | not confirmed | [src](https://indiaspora.org/members/srinivas-narayanan/) |\n| Viralizr | founder / founding team | not confirmed | [src](https://indiaspora.org/members/srinivas-narayanan/) |\n| Tavant | founder / founding team | not confirmed | [src](https://indiaspora.org/members/srinivas-narayanan/) |\n| IBM | former researcher, Almaden | not confirmed | [src](https://indiaspora.org/members/srinivas-narayanan/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Current firm is blank because the first-person departure post names no next employer.\n- Exact OpenAI start/final day, previous employment endpoints, education, check capacity and a stable own-site headshot remain unconfirmed.\n- Exact-name SEC search did not yield a matching relevant filing; unrelated results were excluded.\n- Resolve AI: fetched company launch is dated 2024-09-24, whereas batch rank_reason says 2024-10. Used the displayed source date with an explicit caveat; actual close date remains unconfirmed. [src](https://website-prod.resolve.ai/blog/introducing-resolve-ai)\n- `check_capacity` and `check_size` are blank: no fetched source establishes personal check limits. `fast_promotion: unknown` avoids inferring an investing promotion from an operating-company title.\n\n## Sources\n- [Source 1](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/srinivasnarayanan_im-excited-to-share-that-im-transitioning-activity-7368705215933374466-frjZ)\n- [Source 2](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/srinivasnarayanan_after-3-incredible-years-i-am-leaving-openai-activity-7451025653967114240-NW6Y)\n- [Source 3](https://blog.southparkcommons.com/p/on-building-in-uncertainty-and-future)\n- [Source 4](https://website-prod.resolve.ai/blog/introducing-resolve-ai)\n- [Source 5](https://indiaspora.org/members/srinivas-narayanan/)\n- [Source 6](https://www.linkedin.com/in/srinivasnarayanan/)\n", "person:sriram-viswanathan": "\n# Sriram Viswanathan\n\n## One-line take\nThe Celesta partner on its AI-compute boards (Velaura AI, Tensordyne, Aurascape), who keeps backing the same founder, Rajiv Khemani, from one company to the next [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n\n## Crux\nHe wins with chip founders who already know him from Intel and from earlier Celesta companies, and he stays on the board through several rounds. He is constrained by fund size and LP pressure for distributions: Fund V had $102M a year in [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1987549/000198754925000002/primary_doc.xml), and he says \"DPI is the new IRR\" [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/). He would not chase a hot software round; his deals are hardware and infrastructure.\n\n## Differential insight\n- \"DPI is the new IRR\", and \"Everyone wants DPI\": his read of deep-tech LPs in 2026 [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/).\n- \"there are fewer good companies to go after\" and \"It's a take-all kind of market\" [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/).\n- What he asks of companies now: \"Show me revenue growth. Show me profitability growth.\" [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/)\n- He is a related person on Celesta Capital V; Lip-Bu Tan is not [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1987549/000198754925000002/primary_doc.xml).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. AI chips and compute are on thesis and he enters early (Auradine A, Recogni A), but he is a senior MP at a small fund, not a rising junior, and he does not do labs. Useful as a co-investor signal in AI silicon: Velaura AI, Upscale AI, Tensordyne.\n\n## Background\n- Nearly 20 years at Intel: VP in the Intel Architecture Group, VP at Intel Capital, GM of incubation and innovation; dates not stated [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n- Founded IndusAge Partners, a US-India cross-border venture firm [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n- MBA, UCLA Anderson (1997); degrees from the American College and the Indian Institute of Science [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n- Advisory board, India Semiconductor Mission [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan | |\n| Substack / blog | Celesta Substack (not read) | |\n| X | https://x.com/1sriram | linked from his Celesta page; followers not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/onesriram/ | linked from his Celesta page |\n| GitHub | none | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/techsurge-deep-tech-podcast/id1755173852 | TechSurge: Deep Tech Podcast |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2026-02 — Crunchbase News — interview on deep tech and secondaries — [link](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/) — DPI over IRR; mined\n- ongoing — TechSurge (host) — guests include Sanjay Mehrotra and Stacy Rasgon — [link](https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/techsurge-deep-tech-podcast/id1755173852) — not mined\n\n## How they think\n- Deep tech is winner-take-all with fewer fundable companies, so concentrate on known founders [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/).\n- AI is the hottest area but he also points to biotech and \"pragmatic\" semiconductors [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/).\n- Stated mistakes or passes: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n- The Crunchbase News interview [src](https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/).\n- His Celesta page for the board list [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot reviewed (X not read this pass).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Velaura AI (Auradine) | Series A ($81M) | 2023-05 | co-led; board | Mayfield; DCVC | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n| Tensordyne (Recogni) | Series B ($48.9M) | 2021-02 | led | - | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n| Tensordyne (Recogni) | Series C ($102M) | 2024-02 | co-led; board | GreatPoint | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n| Aurascape | Seed ($12.8M) | 2024-09 | board; Mayfield led | Mayfield | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n| Movandi; Platform9; Baffle | board seats | not stated | - | - | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n\nRound details are from the repo ledger; his page confirms the board seats only.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Khemani chain:** Celesta's portfolio page dates Innovium to 2015, Velaura (Auradine) to 2022, Aurascape to 2024 and Upscale AI to 2025; the last three share founders, and Viswanathan sits on two of the boards [src](https://www.celesta.vc/portfolio) [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan). No first-hand account of the intro was found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Rajiv Khemani | repeat founder (Auradine, Aurascape, Upscale AI) | 2022 | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/portfolio) |\n| Mayfield (Navin Chaddha) | co-lead partner on the Khemani companies | 2023 | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/portfolio) |\n| Lip-Bu Tan; Michael Marks; Nicholas Brathwaite | co-founding managing partners | 2018 or earlier | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1746798/000174679820000003/primary_doc.xml) |\n| Intel Capital | former employer | - | [src](https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan) |\n\n## Open questions\n- When he joined WRVI, and whether he personally sourced Upscale AI.\n- TechSurge episodes where he states views on AI chips have not been read.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.celesta.vc/people/sriram-viswanathan\n- https://news.crunchbase.com/liquidity/deep-tech-investors-secondary-market-viswanathan-celesta/\n- https://www.celesta.vc/portfolio\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1987549/000198754925000002/primary_doc.xml\n", "person:sruthi-ramaswami": "\n# Sruthi Ramaswami\n\n## One-line take\nPartner at ICONIQ Growth. [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/pioneering-the-future-of-data-and-ai-our-partnership-with-databricks)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium. The sourced role and any personally named investments below determine this initial fit; deeper investment judgment is not written in a skeleton page. [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/pioneering-the-future-of-data-and-ai-our-partnership-with-databricks)\n\n## Background\n- Current or most recently verified role: Partner at ICONIQ Growth. [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/pioneering-the-future-of-data-and-ai-our-partnership-with-databricks)\n- Prior employment and education were not confirmed in the retained sources for this skeleton pass.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website / identity source | [Source](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/pioneering-the-future-of-data-and-ai-our-partnership-with-databricks) | Role source |\n| Substack / blog | | Not verified in this pass |\n| X | | Not verified in this pass |\n| LinkedIn | | Not verified in this pass |\n| GitHub | | Not verified in this pass |\n| Podcasts (host) | | Not verified in this pass |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nnothing found\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Read these first\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed in this skeleton pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Writer | Series B ($100M) | 2023-09-18 | yes | WndrCo; Balderton Capital; Insight Partners; Gradient Ventures; Aspect Ventures | [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/bringing-generative-ai-to-the-enterprise-iconiq-growth-invests-in-writer) |\n| Databricks | Series J ($10B at $62B) | 2024-12-17 | no | Thrive Capital (lead); Andreessen Horowitz; DST Global; GIC; Insight Partners; WCM | [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/pioneering-the-future-of-data-and-ai-our-partnership-with-databricks) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| iconiq | at: Partner |  | [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/bringing-generative-ai-to-the-enterprise-iconiq-growth-invests-in-writer) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Confirm promotion dates, prior employment chronology, location, and personal check authority. (checked: [identity source](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/pioneering-the-future-of-data-and-ai-our-partnership-with-databricks); retained deal and relationship sources listed above.)\n\n## Sources\n- [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/pioneering-the-future-of-data-and-ai-our-partnership-with-databricks)\n- [src](https://www.iconiq.com/growth/insights/bringing-generative-ai-to-the-enterprise-iconiq-growth-invests-in-writer)\n", "person:stan-boland": "\n# Stan Boland\n\n## One-line take\nSemiconductor and AI entrepreneur named as an angel in Fractile’s 2024 seed round. [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/?id=56569&print=yes) [src](https://dtl.vc/about/)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh — researcher assessment: Fractile is an early AI-chip investment directly within the owner’s lens. [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/?id=56569&print=yes)\n\n## Background\n- Cambridge physics graduate and member of Cambridge Angels; education and membership dates are not disclosed. [src](https://dtl.vc/about/)\n- Former roles at Icera, NVIDIA, Element 14 and Five AI are identified in Fractile’s seed release; tenure dates are not supplied. [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/?id=56569&print=yes)\n\n- The BritChips host biography also names prior Acorn CEO, Rolls-Royce foreign-exchange and ICL treasury roles; dates are unknown. [src](https://anttheantidote.substack.com/p/britchips-podcast-stan-the-man)\n- Cambridge Angels identifies Boland as Neul’s former CEO. [src](https://www.cambridgeangels.com/positive-exits/2018/1/12/neul-28jp3)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [src](https://dtl.vc/about/) |  |\n| Substack / blog |  | Not found in checked sources |\n| X |  | Not found in checked sources |\n| LinkedIn |  | Not found in checked sources |\n| GitHub |  | Not found in checked sources |\n| Podcasts (host) |  | Not found in checked sources |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2020-12-31 — tech.eu Podcast — Interview Special: Stan Boland (Five) — [src](https://tech.eu/2020/12/31/tech-eu-podcast-interview-special-stan-boland-five) — Catalogued only; not mined.\n- 2025-07-14 — BritChips podcast: Stan the Man — [src](https://anttheantidote.substack.com/p/britchips-podcast-stan-the-man) — Catalogued only; not mined.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Read these first\nNot written yet (skeleton page); media above is a catalogue, not an interpretive reading order.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot researched in this skeleton pass; no follower counts or inferred interaction claims.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fractile | Seed ($15M) | 2024-07-26 | No | Kindred Capital; NATO Innovation Fund; Oxford Science Enterprises; Cocoa; Inovia Capital; Hermann Hauser; Amar Shah | [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/?id=56569&print=yes) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cambridge Angels | at: Angel member | Not found | [src](https://dtl.vc/about/) |\n| Deeptech Labs | at: Independent board member | Not found | [src](https://dtl.vc/about/) |\n| Icera | at: Former team member (specific role and dates unconfirmed) | Not found | [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/?id=56569&print=yes) |\n| NVIDIA | at: Former team member (specific role and dates unconfirmed) | Not found | [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/?id=56569&print=yes) |\n| Element 14 | at: Former team member (specific role and dates unconfirmed) | Not found | [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/?id=56569&print=yes) |\n| Five AI | at: Former team member (specific role and dates unconfirmed) | Not found | [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/?id=56569&print=yes) |\n| Acorn Computers | at: CEO (former) | Not found | [src](https://anttheantidote.substack.com/p/britchips-podcast-stan-the-man) |\n| Rolls-Royce | at: Foreign-exchange role (former) | Not found | [src](https://anttheantidote.substack.com/p/britchips-podcast-stan-the-man) |\n| ICL | at: Group Treasurer (former) | Not found | [src](https://anttheantidote.substack.com/p/britchips-podcast-stan-the-man) |\n| Neul | at: CEO (former) | Not found | [src](https://www.cambridgeangels.com/positive-exits/2018/1/12/neul-28jp3) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Individual check size, check capacity, current location and dated career chronology were not established in the fetched sources.\n- A Deeptech Labs headshot was found at https://dtl.vc/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/stan.jpeg but image retrieval failed; photo is left empty.\n- Fractile’s current site was opened, but its 2024 release was retrieved through the Design & Reuse republication.\n- The BritChips episode is catalogued only; deeper career history and investing insight remain for the insight lane.\n- Check-capacity field is intentionally empty: no fetched source establishes an allowed capacity tier.\n- Blank affiliation dates mean unknown; historical roles are explicitly labeled former. No continuous tenure is inferred.\n\n## Sources\n- [src](https://anttheantidote.substack.com/p/britchips-podcast-stan-the-man)\n- [src](https://dtl.vc/about/)\n- [src](https://fractile.com/)\n- [src](https://tech.eu/2020/12/31/tech-eu-podcast-interview-special-stan-boland-five)\n- [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/?id=56569&print=yes)\n- [src](https://www.cambridgeangels.com/positive-exits/2018/1/12/neul-28jp3)\n", "person:stan-reiss": "\n# Stan Reiss\n\n## One-line take\nThe chip partner at Matrix: in Lightmatter's Series A in 2018 before GV, and on the board of Baya Systems from its Series A.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** one of few early-stage GPs whose stated sector is semiconductors ([team page](https://www.matrix.vc/team)). He takes the first institutional round in hardware companies generalists avoid.\n- **Constraint:** Matrix does not lead late rounds, and chip companies need them; Lightmatter's later rounds were led by others.\n- **Voice:** none found. He has no catalogued essays, podcasts or interviews in this pass.\n\n## The vehicle\nMatrix Partners XII, L.P.: $800M offering, Form D filed 2022-06-17; Stan is one of six managing members ([src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1933208/000193320822000001/primary_doc.xml)). No personal vehicle found. Matrix invests \"from idea through Series A\".\n\n## Differential insight\n- Lightmatter: Matrix co-led the first Series A tranche in 2018; GV joined the extension in February 2019 ([src](https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/)).\n- Baya Systems: he joined the board alongside semiconductor veterans at the Series A ([src](https://convergedigest.com/baya-systems-adds-semiconductor-vets-to-its-board/)).\n- No verbatim quote from him was found; no insight rows are written under his id.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** AI chips and interconnect at seed and Series A (Lightmatter, Baya Systems, Nubis in the firm's portfolio). The kind of early semiconductor investor the lens asks for, at a fund that can write $10M+.\n\n## Background\nNot on the firm page, which gives only sector tags and companies. Not researched further in this pass; nothing here is from memory.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.matrix.vc/team | firm team page |\n| Substack / blog | none found | |\n| X | https://twitter.com/stanreiss | followers not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/stanreiss/ | |\n| GitHub | not checked | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- None found.\n\n## How they think\n- Nothing in his own words found.\n\n## Read these first\n- Nothing of their own writing was found in this pass.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot checked.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lightmatter | Series A (first tranche) | 2018 | Co-lead | Spark Capital | [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/) |\n| Lightmatter | Series A extension | 2019-02-11 | No (GV joined) | GV | [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/) |\n| Baya Systems | Series A | 2024-06 | Board | | [src](https://convergedigest.com/baya-systems-adds-semiconductor-vets-to-its-board/) |\n| Baya Systems | Series B ($36M+) | 2025-01-23 | No | | ledger |\n| Centaur Labs | Series A ($15M) | 2021-09-03 | Matrix led | | ledger; [team page](https://www.matrix.vc/team) |\n| Delos Data, Lightforce, LogRocket, Xtalic | not researched | | | | [team page](https://www.matrix.vc/team) |\n\nFirst institutional rounds: Lightmatter, Baya Systems.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Lightmatter:** Matrix was in before GV; the ledger's lead note says Matrix seeded both Lightmatter and CTRL-labs ahead of GV's 2019 rounds. Intro path not sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Matrix Partners | General Partner | not found | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1933208/000193320822000001/primary_doc.xml) |\n| GV (Erik Nordlander) | Followed Matrix into Lightmatter | 2019 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Any talk or interview on AI chips.\n- Whether he led Nubis and Delos Data, and at what round.\n- His background and join date.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.matrix.vc/team\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1933208/000193320822000001/primary_doc.xml\n- https://globalventuring.com/university/gv-sees-lightmatter-for-series-a-extension/\n- https://convergedigest.com/baya-systems-adds-semiconductor-vets-to-its-board/\n", "person:stanley-druckenmiller": "\n# Stanley Druckenmiller\n\n## One-line take\nA macro billionaire whose private AI book (Etched, Perplexity, Standard Intelligence, Flapping Airplanes, Q.ANT, Reflexivity) gets allocated to him through relationships and his LP stakes. He doesn't source or lead, so he is useful to the owner as a signal of who the \"kids\" network routes into, and as a possible LP, not as a lead investor to follow.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins allocation:** His name is a cap-table asset. Founders list him as an \"angel & advisor\" ([si.inc](https://si.inc/posts/a/)), and Etched puts him on its public angel wall next to Karpathy and Hinton ([etched.com](https://www.etched.com/)). He openly says he outsources AI judgment to younger people. On Morgan Stanley's *Hard Lessons* (early 2026), he said he has \"young superstars in my firm,\" and that in venture \"we've always looked at ... where the kids are going\" (transcribed by [A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)).\n- **The pattern in the deals:** He is late and never leads. The only company where a source calls him the **first** investor is Toggle AI (now Reflexivity), and its co-founder Jan Szilagyi used to work for him at Duquesne ([CNBC 2023-06-06](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html)). Everywhere else he arrives as a listed participant after institutional leads have priced the round:\n  - Perplexity: a \"new investor\" in the April 2024 B1, about 19 months after the 2022 seed ([perplexity.ai](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-launches-enterprise-pro)).\n  - Etched: an unannounced 2024–26 financing, not the 2023 seed ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/)).\n  - Standard Intelligence and Flapping Airplanes: the Series A in both.\n- **What constrains him:** Bandwidth on the private side, which just got worse. Per Barron's, \"Warsh runs the firm's private-equity investments, according to Druckenmiller\" ([Barron's via itiger, 2026-02-02](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848)). Kevin Warsh was nominated Fed chair on 2026-01-30 and confirmed in May 2026. He filed to stop consulting for Duquesne and to divest Juggernaut ([CBS](https://www.cbsnews.com/amp/news/kevin-warsh-net-worth-fed-chair-nominee/)). The person who ran Duquesne's private investing has left, and no successor is named in any source I found.\n- **Public-market discipline over loyalty:** He does trim AI winners and says so on air. He cut Nvidia because it \"tripled in a year.\" By early 2026 he said AI was \"not driving the engine anymore\" and called the cycle \"Maybe 8th inning\" ([NBC/CNBC](https://www.nbcconnecticut.com/news/business/money-report/stanley-druckenmiller-cut-his-nvidia-stake-in-late-march-says-ai-may-be-a-bit-overhyped-short-term/3284318/); [A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)).\n- **What he would never do (inference):** Underwrite a lab on his own technical judgment, or take a board seat. His own word for his edge on AI is \"luck,\" and he credits his \"young partner.\"\n\n## Differential insight\n- **His venture heuristic is talent flow, not technology.** \"that's something we've always looked at in venture is where the kids are going.\" On the same transcript, his 2008–09 Palantir purchase was \"a cool company back then that all the kids wanted to go to.\" ([Morgan Stanley *Hard Lessons*, transcribed by A Letter a Day #320, 2026-03-03](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)). The transcript is third-party (medium confidence).\n- **The Nvidia call came from an unnamed young partner with a Palo Alto AI network.** CNBC: \"My young partner was early with Nvidia.\" \"I didn't even know how to spell it.\" ([CNBC Squawk Box transcript, 2024-05-07](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html)). *Hard Lessons* adds that the partner \"had in people from his AI network in Palo Alto\" and that the signal was engineers moving \"from crypto to AI\" ([A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and); [In Good Company excerpt](https://acquirersmultiple.com/2024/11/stanley-druckenmiller-why-im-bullish-and-cautious-on-ai-investment)). No source names the partner, and it is not Warsh.\n- **He was first money in an AI company only once, and its founder was his own alum.** CNBC calls him \"Toggle's first investor.\" Szilagyi: \"Without him, I'm not sure Giuseppe and I would've really made the leap\" ([CNBC 2023-06-06](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html)). He followed on in the $30M Series B led by Greycroft and Interactive Brokers ([HedgeFundAlpha, 2024-10](https://hedgefundalpha.com/news/reflexivity-raises-30-million-series-b-funding/)).\n- **The Druckenmiller + Warsh pairing is the private-side \"senior/junior\" team (inference from two co-occurrences).**\n  - Barron's says Warsh ran private equity at Duquesne ([src](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848)).\n  - Both men are named together on Hebbia's 2022 round (\"Stanley Druckenmiller, Kevin Warsh, Julius Genachowski\") ([Hebbia blog](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of)).\n  - Both bought into Abstract's management company in 2017 ([Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)).\n  - Abstract's Ramtin Naimi took the second-largest check in Hebbia's early round (same source).\n  - Possible path: the Abstract relationship brought both men into Hebbia. That is inference only. Warsh's 2026 ethics filing lists a Hebbia stake. It is his personal holding, not a disclosed Duquesne position ([CBS](https://www.cbsnews.com/amp/news/kevin-warsh-net-worth-fed-chair-nominee/)).\n- **His first exposure to Flapping Airplanes may have come through an LP stake, not his own sourcing.**\n  - Nova Global, Koko Xs's firm, names him as a backer: \"backed by folks like Marc Andreessen, Henry Kravis, Stan Druckenmiller, Micky Malka\" ([kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/)).\n  - Nova is listed in Flapping's seed, but only by an aggregator ([iq.wiki](https://iq.wiki/wiki/flapping-airplanes)). It also ran an SPV, \"Nova Flappy, LP,\" for the A ([Form D](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2146901/000123191926000844/primary_doc.xml)).\n  - Duquesne Family Office appears directly as an individual backer in the A ([company LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/)).\n- **He co-founded an AI pathology company.** \"we had started this company that basically looks for pathology in prostate cancer,\" and the machines \"could do it as well or better\" ([A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)). The company is not named. Paige (an MSK spin-out with a prostate product) is a candidate only, and Paige's Series A coverage names Breyer, not him ([Becker's](https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/artificial-intelligence/paige-ai-launches-with-25m-in-funding-data-license-agreement-with-memorial-sloan-kettering.html)).\n- **Photonic AI compute through the family office.** Duquesne Family Office joined the second close of Q.ANT's Series A, which brought total funding to $80M ([Tech.eu, 2025-10-30](https://tech.eu/2025/10/30/qant-secures-additional-investment-from-duquesne-family-office-bringing-total-funding-to-80m/)). It is the deal most squarely in the owner's thesis, and it wasn't in the brief.\n- **He reads the cycle as late.** By early 2026 he called AI trading \"disturbingly heated\" and said \"Definitely not early,\" \"Maybe 8th inning\" ([A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)). Yet he kept writing private lab checks (Standard Intelligence in April 2026, Flapping in October 2026). He rotates his public book but keeps adding small private options.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**medium.**\n- **For:** He is on the cap tables of the frontier-AI companies the owner cares about: Etched (AI chips), Standard Intelligence (new lab, Series A), Flapping Airplanes (new lab, $1B A), Q.ANT (photonic AI processors), and Perplexity. He is also an LP in Nova, a concentrated talent-first emerging manager, and was a management-company investor in Abstract. That is exactly the \"LP who backs young concentrated managers\" profile THESIS rates high.\n- **Against:** He doesn't source, lead, price, or take board seats in any AI deal found, and check sizes are never disclosed. As a co-investor signal he lags the leads. His private-side operator, Warsh, has left for the Fed.\n- **Deals that matter to me:** Etched, Standard Intelligence A, Flapping Airplanes A, Q.ANT A, plus the Nova LP stake.\n- **Would he back someone like me?** Plausibly, as an individual LP. The Nova evidence is a single firm-site line, and the Abstract stake was a management-company purchase reached through an Ovitz/Andreessen intro chain ([Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). Both suggest he enters through trusted introducers, not through cold outreach.\n\n## Background\n- Worked at Soros Fund Management; Scott Bessent worked with him there from 1991 to 2000 ([Barron's via itiger, 2026-02-02](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848)).\n- Duquesne Capital's record: an average annual return of about 30% with no losing year over roughly three decades, per secondary coverage ([Hustle Fund](https://www.hustlefund.vc/post/angel-squad-stanley-druckenmiller-investments-the-macro-master-who-generated-30-annual-returns-without-a-single-down-year)).\n- Now runs Duquesne Family Office, which manages his personal fortune. Its listed equity book is about $4B ([Barron's via itiger](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848)).\n- Private capital sits partly in Juggernaut Fund LP, described as \"the private partnership through which Stanley Druckenmiller manages his personal fortune\" ([MarketWatch via itiger, 2026-05-07](https://www.itiger.com/news/2633141932)).\n- I didn't fetch a primary source for education or specific founding years. Open question.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found (Duquesne has no public site found) | 13F filings are the only regular disclosure |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | No personal account found |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Frequent guest: CNBC Squawk Box, Bloomberg, Sohn, In Good Company, Morgan Stanley Hard Lessons |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2023-05 — Sohn Investment Conference — AI is real and could be as impactful as the internet (secondary reporting only, [Trusted Insight](https://www.thetrustedinsight.com/investment-news/stan-druckenmiller-david-tepper-lead-family-offices-betting-on-ai-20230516697/)).\n- 2023-06 — Bloomberg Invest — \"unlike crypto, I think AI is real\"; \"It could be as transformative as the Internet\"; AI names had \"dominated the long portfolio for five or six months\" ([Acquirer's Multiple excerpt](https://acquirersmultiple.com/?p=38566)).\n- 2024-05-07 — CNBC Squawk Box — the young partner's Nvidia call; \"AI might be a little overhyped now but under-hyped long term\" ([CNBC transcript](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html)).\n- 2024-11 — In Good Company with Nicolai Tangen (Norges Bank) — analysts spotted Stanford and MIT engineers moving from crypto to AI; Nvidia was \"picks and shovels\"; the timing was \"just total luck\" ([excerpt](https://acquirersmultiple.com/2024/11/stanley-druckenmiller-why-im-bullish-and-cautious-on-ai-investment); [episode](https://shows.acast.com/in-good-company-with-nicolai-tangen/episodes/highlights-stan-druckenmiller)).\n- 2025-01 — CNBC — AI adopters that cut costs will outperform; expects AGI by 2035 (secondary: [X clip post](https://x.com/timothyhliu5/status/1882242636846928227)).\n- 2026-02/03 — Morgan Stanley *Hard Lessons* with Iliana Bouzali — \"where the kids are going,\" Palantir 2008–09, the prostate-pathology company he co-started, and AI as \"8th inning\" ([transcription](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and); [MS page](https://www.morganstanley.com/insights/videos/hard-lessons/duquesne-stan-druckenmiller-iliana-bouzali), timed out when fetched).\n\n## How they think\n- **Theses:** AI \"could rhyme with the internet\": overhyped short term, underhyped long term ([CNBC 2024](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html)). He started with \"picks and shovels\" (Nvidia), then moved to infrastructure bottlenecks (memory, Broadcom, TSMC) and later rotated out (Q2 2026 13F: exited Micron, Intel, Broadcom) ([24/7 Wall St](https://247wallst.com/investing/2026/08/20/billionaire-stanley-druckenmiller-dumped-micron-intel-and-broadcom-here-are-the-ai-infrastructure-stocks-he-bought-instead/)).\n- **Contrarian views:** He says anyone predicting AI job losses \"with conviction suffers from arrogance\" ([A Letter a Day #320](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and)).\n- **Calls right:** Nvidia bought in fall 2022, before ChatGPT ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html)); Palantir privately in 2008–09.\n- **Calls wrong (self-admitted):** Selling all his Nvidia, which he called a \"big mistake\" (secondary: [NBC Chicago](https://nbcchicago.com/news/business/money-report/stanley-druckenmiller-says-hes-licking-my-wounds-from-selling-nvidia-too-soon/3576080)).\n\n## Read these first\n1. Morgan Stanley *Hard Lessons* transcript (A Letter a Day #320): his venture heuristic, the young-partner AI story, and his current AI caution. https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and\n2. CNBC Squawk Box transcript, 2024-05-07: the Nvidia origin story in his own words. https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html\n3. CNBC on Toggle AI (2023-06-06): the one AI company where he was first money, and why. https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html\n4. Barron's, \"The Druckenmiller Connection\" (2026-02-02): Warsh's private-equity role at Duquesne. https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no personal social account, and his public voice is TV, conference and podcast interviews.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Palantir | private (undisclosed) | 2008–09 (his account) | no evidence | not named | [Hard Lessons transcript](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and) |\n| Toggle AI / Reflexivity | first investor (pre-seed or seed, undated) | before 2023-06 | first money; lead not stated | not named | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html) |\n| Reflexivity | Series B, $30M | 2024-10-28 | no (Greycroft, Interactive Brokers) | Greg Coffey, General Catalyst, SoftBank LatAm | [HedgeFundAlpha](https://hedgefundalpha.com/news/reflexivity-raises-30-million-series-b-funding/) |\n| Hebbia | Series A, $30M (blog also says some \"joined the seed\") | 2022-09-27 | no (Index, Mike Volpi) | Radical, Kevin Warsh, Jerry Yang, Ram Shriram, Alexandr Wang, Pieter Abbeel, others | [Hebbia blog](https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of) |\n| Perplexity | Series B1, $62.7M at ~$1.04B | 2024-04-23 | no (Daniel Gross) | Garry Tan, Dylan Field, Brad Gerstner, Jeff Bezos, NVIDIA, Karpathy, IVP, NEA | [Perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-launches-enterprise-pro) |\n| Etched | undisclosed (one of four unannounced financings 2024–26) | by 2026-06-30 | no | Thiel; Ribbit, Jump, HRT, Two Sigma, Stripes on cap table | [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/), [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/) |\n| Q.ANT (photonic AI processors) | Series A second close (total $80M) | 2025-10-30 | no (Cherry, UVC, imec.xpand existing leads); goldi.ai labels Duquesne \"Lead\" (aggregator conflict) | L-Bank, TRUMPF, Verve, others | [Tech.eu](https://tech.eu/2025/10/30/qant-secures-additional-investment-from-duquesne-family-office-bringing-total-funding-to-80m/) |\n| Standard Intelligence | Series A, $75M | 2026-04-30 | no (Sequoia, Spark) | Karpathy, Milan Kovac | [si.inc](https://si.inc/posts/a/) |\n| Flapping Airplanes | Series A, $1B (via Duquesne Family Office) | 2026-10-21 | no (Index, Lux) | Kravis, Malka, Steven Cohen, Nova Global (SPV) | [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/) |\n| Precision Neuroscience (BCI) | Series C / Series D | 2024-12 / 2026-09 | participant | Pershing Square, B Capital (D) | aggregator only: [goldi.ai](https://goldi.ai/intelligence/vc/duquesne-family-office), [Tracxn](https://tracxn.com/d/single-family-office/duquesne-family-office/__Xt4unrgbtvrRfmvJIBn9r4N29E_yLyiTH51ZVzPQNxI) |\n| Unnamed AI prostate-pathology company | co-founder (\"we had started\") | undated | n/a | not named | [Hard Lessons transcript](https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and) |\n| **LP / GP-stake** Nova Global (Koko Xs) | LP / backer | by 2026 | n/a | Andreessen, Kravis, Malka | [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/) |\n| **GP-stake** Abstract Ventures management company | 20% for $10M round (his share undisclosed) | 2017 | no | Andreessen, Dixon, Sacks, Rabois, Hartz, Ackman, Warsh, Ovitz | [Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n\nExcluded: Iambic Therapeutics. The October 2026 IPO drew a non-binding indication of interest that Duquesne and ARK shared ([Renaissance Capital](https://www.renaissancecapital.com/IPO-Center/News/122239/small-molecule-biotech-iambic-therapeutics-priced-ipo-at-16-the-midpoint)). That is public-market, not venture.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Toggle / Reflexivity:** Jan Szilagyi joined Duquesne right after his economics PhD and spent most of his career there. He then co-founded Toggle with Giuseppe Sette, with Druckenmiller as first investor ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html)). This is the alum-spinout path, the only \"first in\" case found.\n- **Nvidia (public, but it explains his AI sourcing):** In fall 2022 a young partner, who had brought in people from an AI network in Palo Alto, told him to buy Nvidia. He bought \"a pretty good chunk,\" and ChatGPT launched weeks later ([CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html); [In Good Company excerpt](https://acquirersmultiple.com/2024/11/stanley-druckenmiller-why-im-bullish-and-cautious-on-ai-investment)).\n- **Abstract (2017):** Naimi's intro chain ran Cyan Banister → Kevin Hartz → Rabois and Dixon → Andreessen → Ovitz → Ackman, Warsh, Druckenmiller, Sacks ([Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/)). It shows that his GP-side bets come through elite intro chains.\n- **Flapping Airplanes A (2026-10):** Duquesne appears among the A's individual backers alongside two other Nova backers, Kravis and Malka. Nova ran a dedicated SPV. Whether Nova brought him in or he brought Nova in is unknown (see `firms/nova-global.md`).\n- **Etched:** I couldn't find when he entered or how. The Etched seed was Primary-led in 2023-03. He is tied only to the \"four unannounced financings\" ([TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kevin Warsh | Duquesne partner/adviser; ran private equity; co-investor in Hebbia and Abstract; left to become Fed chair in 2026 | 2011 | [Barron's via itiger](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848), [CBS](https://www.cbsnews.com/amp/news/kevin-warsh-net-worth-fed-chair-nominee/) |\n| Unnamed \"young partner\" (Duquesne) | Brought the Palo Alto AI network and the Nvidia call | 2022 | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html) |\n| Jan Szilagyi | Former Duquesne employee; Druckenmiller was first investor in his company | pre-2018 | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html) |\n| Koko Xs / Nova Global | LP / backer | by 2026 | [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/) |\n| Ramtin Naimi / Abstract | Management-company investor | 2017 | [Colossus](https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/) |\n| Scott Bessent | Soros Fund Management colleague | 1991–2000 | [Barron's via itiger](https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848) |\n| Henry Kravis, Micky Malka | Fellow Nova backers and co-participants in the Flapping A | 2026 | [kokoxs.com](https://kokoxs.com/), [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/) |\n| Andrej Karpathy | Co-angel at Etched, Standard Intelligence and Perplexity | 2024– | [si.inc](https://si.inc/posts/a/), [etched.com](https://www.etched.com/), [Perplexity](https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-launches-enterprise-pro) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is the \"young partner\" with the Palo Alto AI network? They likely route the private AI deals too. Does that partner now run private investing after Warsh's exit?\n- Which Etched round did he enter, at what price, and through whom (Ribbit/Malka? Nova? Thiel?)?\n- Hebbia: did he join the seed or the Series A? The blog folds both into one paragraph, and `companies/hebbia.md` records Series A.\n- Which company is the prostate-pathology business he \"started\"? Paige is a candidate, unconfirmed.\n- Was the Flapping exposure Duquesne direct, through Nova's SPV, or both?\n- Did Duquesne lead any Q.ANT tranche (goldi.ai says \"Lead\" in 2025-01), or only join the second close (Tech.eu)?\n- Is he an LP in other emerging AI managers besides Nova and the Abstract management company?\n- Check sizes: none disclosed anywhere.\n\n## Sources\n- https://aletteraday.substack.com/p/letter-320-stan-druckenmiller-and\n- https://www.morganstanley.com/insights/videos/hard-lessons/duquesne-stan-druckenmiller-iliana-bouzali (timed out)\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/07/cnbc-exclusive-cnbc-transcript-billionaire-investor-stanley-druckenmiller-speaks-with-cnbcs-squawk-box-today.html\n- https://www.nbcconnecticut.com/news/business/money-report/stanley-druckenmiller-cut-his-nvidia-stake-in-late-march-says-ai-may-be-a-bit-overhyped-short-term/3284318/\n- https://acquirersmultiple.com/2024/11/stanley-druckenmiller-why-im-bullish-and-cautious-on-ai-investment\n- https://acquirersmultiple.com/?p=38566\n- https://acquirersmultiple.com/?p=43973\n- https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/06/a-harvard-econ-phd-whiz-teaching-generative-ai-to-invest-for-everyone.html\n- https://hedgefundalpha.com/news/reflexivity-raises-30-million-series-b-funding/\n- https://hebbia.com/blog/hebbia-raises-usd30-million-led-by-index-ventures-to-launch-the-future-of\n- https://www.perplexity.ai/hub/blog/perplexity-launches-enterprise-pro\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/30/nvidia-competitor-etched-hits-5b-valuation-1b-in-sales-for-ai-chip/\n- https://www.etched.com/\n- https://si.inc/posts/a/\n- https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518737960943513600/\n- https://tech.eu/2025/10/30/qant-secures-additional-investment-from-duquesne-family-office-bringing-total-funding-to-80m/\n- https://goldi.ai/intelligence/vc/duquesne-family-office (aggregator)\n- https://tracxn.com/d/single-family-office/duquesne-family-office/__Xt4unrgbtvrRfmvJIBn9r4N29E_yLyiTH51ZVzPQNxI (aggregator)\n- https://kokoxs.com/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2146901/000123191926000844/primary_doc.xml\n- https://iq.wiki/wiki/flapping-airplanes (aggregator)\n- https://colossus.com/article/ramtin-naimi-man-hot-hand/\n- https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2608830848\n- https://www.itiger.com/news/2633141932\n- https://www.cbsnews.com/amp/news/kevin-warsh-net-worth-fed-chair-nominee/\n- https://www.renaissancecapital.com/IPO-Center/News/122239/small-molecule-biotech-iambic-therapeutics-priced-ipo-at-16-the-midpoint\n- https://247wallst.com/investing/2026/08/20/billionaire-stanley-druckenmiller-dumped-micron-intel-and-broadcom-here-are-the-ai-infrastructure-stocks-he-bought-instead/\n- https://nbcchicago.com/news/business/money-report/stanley-druckenmiller-says-hes-licking-my-wounds-from-selling-nvidia-too-soon/3576080\n- https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/artificial-intelligence/paige-ai-launches-with-25m-in-funding-data-license-agreement-with-memorial-sloan-kettering.html\n- https://www.hustlefund.vc/post/angel-squad-stanley-druckenmiller-investments-the-macro-master-who-generated-30-annual-returns-without-a-single-down-year\n\nBackfilled 2026-11-16: known_for;photo\n", "person:stefan-cohen": "\n# Stefan Cohen\n\n## One-line take\nBain Capital Crypto managing director focused on crypto infrastructure and privacy. [src](https://www.baincapital.com/people/stefan-cohen)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow: Bain Capital Crypto managing director focused on crypto infrastructure and privacy. [src](https://www.baincapital.com/people/stefan-cohen)\n\n## Background\nBain Capital says Cohen joined in 2016 and is a managing director who leads crypto investments; it lists prior Turbonomic and IBM strategy work. [src](https://www.baincapital.com/people/stefan-cohen)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [Source page](https://www.baincapital.com/people/stefan-cohen) | Source reviewed for this skeleton. |\n| Substack / blog |  | Not confirmed in reviewed sources. |\n| X |  | Not confirmed in reviewed sources. |\n| LinkedIn |  | Not confirmed in reviewed sources. |\n| GitHub |  | Not confirmed in reviewed sources. |\n| Podcasts (host) |  | Not confirmed in reviewed sources. |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNo source-backed appearance catalogued in this skeleton pass.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Read these first\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed in this skeleton pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\nNo source-backed personal financing attribution was established in this pass.\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bain Capital Crypto | at: Managing Director | 2016 | [src](https://www.baincapital.com/people/stefan-cohen) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Personal check capacity, a direct durable headshot, and additional personally attributed financings were not established. (checked: [source page](https://www.baincapital.com/people/stefan-cohen))\n- Prior-employment dates and promotion chronology remain incomplete where the reviewed sources do not date them. (checked: [source page](https://www.baincapital.com/people/stefan-cohen))\n\n## Sources\n- [Source 1](https://www.baincapital.com/people/stefan-cohen)\n", "person:stefan-dyckerhoff": "\n# Stefan Dyckerhoff\n\n## One-line take\nThe Sutter Hill MD who quietly builds the AI data-center networking and interconnect stack (Astera Labs, Enfabrica, Celero, nEye, Aria Networks), often by incubating the company and sometimes by running it himself.\n\n## Crux\nAn ex-Juniper systems executive (employee #33) who invests the Sutter Hill way: incubate in-house, put a partner in the CEO seat if needed, then lead or overfund every follow-on. He ran SiFive from inception to Aug 2017 [src](https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo) and Lacework from Nov 2017 [src](https://www.globalsecuritymag.fr/Lacework-Introduces-New-Chief,20171107,74972.html) without leaving the firm. His edge is technical depth in networking and silicon, plus a network of chip operators (Apstra, Inphi/Marvell, ClariPhy). His constraint is a low public profile: Sutter Hill rarely does press, so almost all his on-record words are press-release quotes. He would not be a passive minority investor in a lab-style mega-seed. His pattern is owning the company from creation.\n\n## Differential insight\n- He was **SiFive's founding CEO**, holding \"the top spot at the company since its inception\" until Aug 2017 [src](https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo). Most deal databases only record Sutter Hill as SiFive's first investor.\n- 2016 thesis on custom silicon: \"We want to radically drive down human involvement in design costs\". He argued processor IP is overvalued and the SoC implementation is the hard part [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/).\n- Hidden link: \"deeply involved with P.A. Semi prior to the Apple acquisition\" [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/).\n- Timing read in 2016: a \"definite uptick in developer registration activity\" for RISC-V after SoftBank bought ARM [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/).\n- Check-size habit: in Atmosic's $72M round (2022) Sutter Hill chose to \"double down with a super pro-rata investment\" [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/51273/atmosic-funding.html). It led Atmosic's B, the 2022 round and the 2025 D [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2025030303/atmosic-technologies-secures-40m-funding-led-by-sutter-hill-ventures/).\n- Decision rule (Aria, 2026): conviction is \"grounded in the technology, the momentum, and above all, the founders\". Customers were \"already ordering\" before the $125M round [src](https://datacentrenews.uk/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million).\n- Technical bet (nEye, 2026): away from \"complex mechanical assemblies to a foundry-compatible wafer-scale process\". He sizes optical circuit switching at $3B+ within three years [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026041401/neye-ai-secures-80-million-series-c-to-scale-optical-circuit-switching-for-ai-infrastructure/).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** He is the Sutter Hill partner closest to AI semiconductors and compute: Astera Labs (board since Nov 2019, before the AI boom), Enfabrica (board since 2020 per Astera IR bio, secondary), Celero (seed and A, 2024), nEye (led C, 2026) and Aria Networks (led $125M first round, 2026). Many of these rounds sit at the $8M-$125M scale where a $20M co-investment is realistic. Repeat co-investors Atreides, Valor and CapitalG show where the follow-on capital comes from.\n\n## Background\n- Duke (BS), Stanford (MS) [src](https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/profile/stefan-dyckerhoff/).\n- Juniper Networks from 1997 as employee #33 on the founding engineering team. Rose to EVP of the Platform Systems Division [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=681511).\n- Lacework's 2017 release also says he led Cisco's enterprise security and routing business before Juniper [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/lacework-names-stefan-dyckerhoff-chief-executive-officer-300550869.html). The dates conflict with TechCrunch (see Open questions).\n- Joined Sutter Hill as Managing Director in Oct 2012, alongside Sam Pullara [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=681511).\n- SiFive founding CEO, 2015 to Aug 2017 [src](https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo).\n- Lacework President and CEO from Nov 2017. Dan Hubbard took over in 2019 [src](https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/security-industry-veteran-dan-hubbard-named-ceo-of-lacework-300862324.html).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | nothing found (Sutter Hill site has no bios) | |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found; one 2018 Infosecurity Magazine column [src](https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/opinions/questions-ceo-moving-cloud/) | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | https://linkedin.com/in/stefandyckerhoff | not checked |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2016-10: SemiWiki interview (SiFive strategy) [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/). Customization thesis, design-cost automation, China deprioritised.\n- 2019-12: RISC-V Summit talk \"How RISC-V made the Quick Jump from Academia to Industry... a Venture Capital perspective\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CfFMLG1hF6U). Not mined: YouTube blocked transcript access and the slides link on riscv.org returns 404.\n- 2026-04: Aria Networks launch video [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LjMWrTDkKrA). His appearance is unconfirmed.\n- No podcasts found.\n\n## How they think\n- Compute that benefits from customization beats general-purpose IP. Automate the design labor [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/).\n- Platform shifts break the adjacent layer: cloud broke security (Lacework), and AI clusters break networking and optics (Celero, nEye, Aria) [src](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/lacework-names-stefan-dyckerhoff-chief-executive-officer-300550869.html).\n- Funds winners hard after the first check: super pro-rata in Atmosic, and kept investing in Astera's C and D.\n- Calls right: Astera Labs (backed in 2019-2020, IPO 2024). Lacework rose high, then sold for far less than its peak (not covered here).\n\n## Read these first\n1. SemiWiki 2016 interview: the only long-form record of his thinking [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/).\n2. Infosecurity Magazine column (2018): his operator stance [src](https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/opinions/questions-ceo-moving-cloud/).\n3. Aria Networks launch coverage (2026): his current rule for leading a big round [src](https://datacentrenews.uk/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million).\n4. nEye Series C release (2026) [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026041401/neye-ai-secures-80-million-series-c-to-scale-optical-circuit-switching-for-ai-infrastructure/).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. No active X or Substack presence was located.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SiFive | Series A ($5M) | 2015-09 | yes | | [src](https://seedtable.com/companies/sifive/funding-rounds) (aggregator) |\n| SiFive | Series C ($50.6M) | 2018-04 | co-lead | Spark Capital | [src](https://www.sifive.com/posts/2018/04/02/sifive-secures-50-million-funding-to-advance-risc-v-based-semiconductors/) |\n| Lacework | Series B ($24M) | 2018-08 | Sutter Hill led | Liberty Global Ventures, AME Cloud Ventures | [src](https://zingy-fr.netlify.app/fr/press-release/lacework-closes-24-million-series-b-financing) |\n| Atmosic | Series B ($28.5M) | 2019-12 | Sutter Hill led | Clear Ventures, Walden | [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/47306/atmosic-funding.html) |\n| Astera Labs | Series B (board Nov 2019) | 2020-04 | | Intel Capital, Avigdor Willenz | [src](https://dev.aiwire.net/2020/04/22/astera-labs-closes-series-b-funding-announces-manufacturing-partnerships/) |\n| Astera Labs | Series C ($50M at $950M) | 2021-10 | no (Fidelity) | Atreides, Valor | [src](https://www.businesswirechina.com/en/news/48219.html) |\n| Atmosic | Undisclosed ($72M, super pro-rata) | 2022-01 | Sutter Hill led | | [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/51273/atmosic-funding.html) |\n| Celero Communications | Seed + Series A ($40M) | 2024 | Sutter Hill led | Valor, Atreides, Maverick Silicon | [src](https://pulse2.com/celero-communications-140-million-raised-to-expand-ai-infrastructure-connectivity) |\n| Atmosic | Series D ($40M) | 2025-03 | Sutter Hill led | Clear Ventures | [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2025030303/atmosic-technologies-secures-40m-funding-led-by-sutter-hill-ventures/) |\n| Aria Networks | Series A ($125M) | 2026-04 | yes | Atreides, Valor, Eclipse | [src](https://datacentrenews.uk/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million) |\n| nEye | Series C ($80M) | 2026-04 | yes | CapitalG, M12, Socratic Partners | [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026041401/neye-ai-secures-80-million-series-c-to-scale-optical-circuit-switching-for-ai-infrastructure/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **SiFive**: Sutter Hill funded the Berkeley RISC-V team, and Dyckerhoff served as CEO from inception. The first win was a 2-month custom-core project for Microsemi's end customer [src](https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/). He handed over to Naveed Sherwani in Aug 2017 and kept the board seat [src](https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo).\n- **Lacework**: incubated at Sutter Hill. He became CEO in Nov 2017 while remaining MD [src](https://www.globalsecuritymag.fr/Lacework-Introduces-New-Chief,20171107,74972.html). Sutter Hill then led the $24M B at about 20 production customers [src](https://zingy-fr.netlify.app/fr/press-release/lacework-closes-24-million-series-b-financing).\n- **Aria Networks**: founders Mansour Karam and Subhachandra Chandra became Sutter Hill EIRs after the Apstra exit to Juniper. Sutter Hill then led the $125M first round, and Dyckerhoff joined the board with Atreides' Gavin Baker [src](https://www.implicator.ai/aria-networks-sells-the-same-chips-as-arista-its-125-million-bet-is-the-software/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sutter Hill Ventures | Managing Director; 10-25% owner of adviser | 2012 | [src](https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162959/PDF/162959.pdf) |\n| Sam Pullara | Joined Sutter Hill as MD the same day | 2012 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/?p=681511) |\n| Gavin Baker (Atreides) | Co-board member at Aria; Atreides also co-invests in Astera and Celero | 2026 | [src](https://datacentrenews.uk/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million) |\n| Rajeev Madhavan (Clear Ventures) | Co-investor and co-board at Atmosic | 2019 | [src](https://us.design-reuse.com/news/51273/atmosic-funding.html) |\n| Mansour Karam | Sutter Hill EIR, then Aria founder | 2021+ | [src](https://www.implicator.ai/aria-networks-sells-the-same-chips-as-arista-its-125-million-bet-is-the-software/) |\n| Krste Asanovic / SiFive founders | Ran SiFive as CEO with them | 2015 | [src](https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Cisco stint: the Lacework release puts it before Juniper, but TechCrunch says he joined Juniper from Stanford in 1997. When did he work at Cisco?\n- What did he say in the 2019 RISC-V Summit VC talk? The video exists but no transcript was reachable, and the slides URL is dead.\n- An eWeek 2018 interview reportedly has his Lacework customer count and competitor view, but it sits behind Cloudflare and was not read.\n- Enfabrica board role (from 2020 per Astera IR, secondary): did he lead its Series A?\n- Did he sit on ThousandEyes, Vera or Benu boards? These rest only on aggregator listings.\n\n## Sources\n- https://semiwiki.com/ip/sifive/6257-sifive-execs-share-ideas-on-their-risc-v-strategy/\n- https://www.sifive.com/press/sifive-appoints-naveed-sherwani-as-ceo\n- https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/lacework-names-stefan-dyckerhoff-chief-executive-officer-300550869.html\n- https://www.globalsecuritymag.fr/Lacework-Introduces-New-Chief,20171107,74972.html\n- https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/opinions/questions-ceo-moving-cloud/\n- https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/profile/stefan-dyckerhoff/\n- https://zingy-fr.netlify.app/fr/press-release/lacework-closes-24-million-series-b-financing\n- https://techcrunch.com/?p=681511\n- https://us.design-reuse.com/news/47306/atmosic-funding.html\n- https://us.design-reuse.com/news/51273/atmosic-funding.html\n- https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2025030303/atmosic-technologies-secures-40m-funding-led-by-sutter-hill-ventures/\n- https://dev.aiwire.net/2020/04/22/astera-labs-closes-series-b-funding-announces-manufacturing-partnerships/\n- https://www.businesswirechina.com/en/news/48219.html\n- https://pulse2.com/celero-communications-140-million-raised-to-expand-ai-infrastructure-connectivity\n- https://datacentrenews.uk/story/aria-launches-deep-networking-platform-with-usd-125-million\n- https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026041401/neye-ai-secures-80-million-series-c-to-scale-optical-circuit-switching-for-ai-infrastructure/\n- https://www.implicator.ai/aria-networks-sells-the-same-chips-as-arista-its-125-million-bet-is-the-software/\n- https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/reports/ADV/162959/PDF/162959.pdf\n", "person:stefan-williams": "\n# Stefan Williams\n\n## One-line take\nThe acquirer route into Snowflake: he runs M&A and built the venture arm, then handed day-to-day Ventures to Harsha Kapre.\n\n## Crux\nWilliams is a corp-dev operator who has done over 25 acquisitions and investments across Genesys, ServiceNow and Snowflake. He matters as the buyer: Snowflake's exits for venture-backed data and AI companies (Streamlit, Datavolo, Observe) go through him. The constraint is Snowflake's product roadmap; he buys or backs what grows platform consumption. He is not personally named on any AI venture round found.\n\n## Differential insight\n- Snowflake Ventures was built as a corp-dev tool: GCV says he set it up after approval in 2020 to speed adoption of the Data Cloud, and he still oversees M&A alongside it [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-stefan-williams/?format=md).\n- Seat lineage: ServiceNow corp dev (his old seat) later lost Vishal Kumar Gupta (2025-10) and Philip Kirk (to Databricks, 2026-04); Williams left that team in 2019 [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/servicenow-investment-team-shrinks-kirk/?format=md).\n- No verbatim quotes found beyond his bio; no insight rows written.\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow. He is the exit path, not a seed or lab investor. Useful for understanding who buys AI data-infra companies. Deals that matter: Streamlit ($800M acquisition), Observe acquisition (2026), Datavolo.\n\n## Background\n- BSc Financial Economics, University of Leicester; MSc Finance, Warwick Business School [src](https://snowflake.com/en/blog/authors/stefan-williams/).\n- Investment banking at Martinwolf, then corp dev at Genesys [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/corporate-venturer/powerlist-2023-stefan-williams/?format=md).\n- About three years in corp dev and ventures at ServiceNow, to 2019 [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-stefan-williams/?format=md).\n- Joined Snowflake late 2019 as head of corp dev; founded Snowflake Ventures 2020; title expanded to include startups by 2025 [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-stefan-williams/?format=md).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://snowflake.com/en/blog/authors/stefan-williams/ | One post (2025-09-24, Snowflake for Startups) |\n| Substack / blog | Snowflake blog | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked (LinkedIn not fetched) | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Undated — Snowflake podcast — \"Stefan Williams and Uri May\" — https://www.snowflake.com/en/podcast/stefan-williams-and-uri-may/ — not mined.\n\n## How they think\n- Uses investment as a go-to-market lever (Startup Accelerator, Startup Challenge with NYSE, $200M accelerator commitment).\n- M&A favours tuck-ins that add AI data-pipeline capability (Datavolo) or app layers (Streamlit).\n\n## Read these first\n- \"Snowflake for Startups\" launch post (2025-09-24): https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/authors/stefan-williams/\n- GCV Powerlist 2025 profile: https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-stefan-williams/?format=md\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: no active public X or Substack presence located (checked 2026-11-12). Their public voice is firm-blog bylines and announcement quotes.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Streamlit | Acquisition ($800M) | (year not in source) | yes (acquirer) | | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-stefan-williams/?format=md) |\n| Datavolo | Acquisition | November (year not in source) | yes (acquirer) | | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-stefan-williams/?format=md) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found about how specific deals were sourced.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Harsha Kapre | Now leads the Snowflake Ventures unit Williams founded | | [src](https://globalventuring.com/people/powerlist-2026-harsha-kapre-snowflake-ventures/?format=md) |\n| Snowflake Ventures | at: Founder | 2020 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-stefan-williams/?format=md) |\n| Snowflake | at: Head of Corp Dev, then VP Corp Dev, Ventures and startups | 2019 | [src](https://snowflake.com/en/blog/authors/stefan-williams/) |\n| ServiceNow | at: Corp dev and ventures | ~2016-2019 | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-stefan-williams/?format=md) |\n| Genesys | at: Corp dev | | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/corporate-venturer/powerlist-2023-stefan-williams/?format=md) |\n| Martinwolf | at: Investment banking | | [src](https://globalventuring.com/corporate/corporate-venturer/powerlist-2023-stefan-williams/?format=md) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Whether he still formally oversees Snowflake Ventures or only M&A.\n- His role in the 2024 Mistral strategic investment.\n- Exact years at Genesys and ServiceNow.\n- Headshot: the Snowflake author image blocks automated checks (HTTP 403), so no photo is set.\n\n## Sources\n- https://snowflake.com/en/blog/authors/stefan-williams/\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/awards/powerlist-2025-stefan-williams/?format=md\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/people/powerlist-2024-stefan-williams/?format=md\n- https://globalventuring.com/corporate/corporate-venturer/powerlist-2023-stefan-williams/?format=md\n", "person:sten-tamkivi": "\n# Sten Tamkivi\n\n## One-line take\nPlural partner and Skaala partner. [src](https://www.pluralplatform.com/team)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow: the sourced lead in this batch is Karman+, a space-mining company rather than a core frontier-AI target. [src](https://www.pluralplatform.com/team)\n\n## Background\n- Plural lists Tamkivi as a partner; Skaala also lists him in its partnership. [src](https://www.pluralplatform.com/team) [src](https://skaala.org/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [Plural team](https://www.pluralplatform.com/team) | Firm roster |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNot catalogued in this skeleton pass.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Read these first\nNot prioritized yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed (skeleton page).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Plural | Partner |  | [src](https://www.pluralplatform.com/team) |\n| Skaala | Partner |  | [src](https://skaala.org/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Investment attribution, start dates, photo, and individual check capacity are not established by the reviewed sources.\n\n## Sources\n- [Plural team](https://www.pluralplatform.com/team)\n- [Skaala](https://skaala.org/)\n", "person:stephane-kurgan": "\n# Stephane Kurgan\n\n## One-line take\nAn operator venture partner at Index London (ex-COO of King, the Candy Crush maker). They add a senior name to applied-AI rounds that a full partner runs day to day, here Scope (industrial inspection AI, $20M, 2026) and Arondite (defence AI, $12M seed, 2025). They are not a route into labs, pre-training or compute. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** Operating credibility at scale. The Index bio credits them with helping oversee King's IPO and its later sale to Activision Blizzard, after earlier roles across sales, product, finance and management, plus McKinsey. Founders who want scaling help get a former COO on the board. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- **Constraint:** The title is Venture Partner. On both AI deals they co-sign with Bastian Hasslinger, an Index partner who is on every European AI-native automation deal in that cluster. That suggests Hasslinger runs these deals and Kurgan is the senior operator voice (an inference from the bylines, not stated by Index). [Scope](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) [Arondite](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/)\n- **What they back:** Applied AI in physical or regulated industries, where the founders learned the trade by hand, and consumer gaming (Dream Games, Bigger Games), which fits the King background. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- **What they'd never do (inferred):** Nothing on record suggests they would lead a pre-product research lab or chip company. No such deal is named on their page.\n\n## Differential insight\nTheir public footprint is thin. There is no X account on the Index page, and I found no podcast or talk in this pass because the web-search budget was used up (see Open questions). Everything below comes from the two Index Perspectives they co-signed and the trade press on those deals.\n- **Founder archetype (Scope, with Hasslinger):** \"There's a type of founder we keep coming back to at Index.\" The passage describes founders obsessed with a problem they found themselves. Scope's founders cold-called inspection firms and went on inspections. [BusinessCloud, 2026-05-20](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/)\n- **Trajectory over metrics:** The Scope post says \"that trajectory, more than any single metric, is what convinced us\". It is an explicit statement that they bet on the founders' rate of improvement, not on traction numbers. [Index, 2026-05-19](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/)\n- **Index tracked Scope before investing:** The post says \"we've had a front-row seat to their evolution\". Index watched the company over time and was not the first money. [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/)\n- **Timing (Scope): late, not first.** Susa Ventures and Entrepreneurs First were the earlier backers. CEO Jonny Low said they \"have doubled down in every round\". Index came in at the $20M round (£15M, which press calls a Series A). [BusinessCloud](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/)\n- **Timing (Arondite): known early, but second in.** Index says \"We've known Arondite since inception\". Concept Ventures still led an unannounced $2.25M pre-seed in 2024, before Index put about $10M into the $12.25M seed. [Index, 2025-05-01](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/) [Calibre Defence, 2025-05-06](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/)\n- **Industrial-data thesis:** Scope is pitched as \"a live intelligence layer for physical infrastructure\". The AI workflows save inspectors time first, then build a proprietary dataset on how assets degrade. [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Low.** Their AI deals are applied, not frontier:\n- Scope: AI-native automation for industrial inspection, closest to the Serval end of THESIS.md, but a Series A with Index arriving after Susa Ventures and EF.\n- Arondite: defence AI and human-machine teaming.\n\nThey have no pre-training, new-lab or AI-chip deals. Hasslinger and Hannah Seal (both profiled) are the better Index London contacts for AI-native automation. Kurgan matters mainly as a co-signer who validates operator-heavy, physical-world AI companies, and possibly as a gaming and consumer connection given the King background.\n\n## Background\n- BSc Economics, Université Libre de Bruxelles; diploma in International Relations, Johns Hopkins SAIS; MBA, INSEAD. Dates are not on the firm page. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- McKinsey & Co. consultant; then sales, product, finance and senior-management roles at European tech companies. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- COO of King Digital Entertainment, which made Candy Crush Saga. The bio says they helped oversee the IPO and the later acquisition by Activision Blizzard. Dates are not given on the page. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- Joined Index Ventures as Venture Partner in 2020. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/ | Index team page, 2026-11-10 |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Only co-authored Index Perspectives |\n| X | nothing found | Index page links only the firm account |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/stephane-kurgan-149181 | Linked from the Index page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Nothing found in this pass. Web search was unavailable (budget exhausted), so this is unverified, not a confirmed absence.\n\n## How they think\n- **Founders who earned the insight by hand.** Scope's founders cold-called and joined inspections, and Arondite's CEO came from the British Army, Palantir and Helsing. [Scope](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) [Calibre](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/)\n- **Trajectory over metrics** (Scope post).\n- **Purpose-driven leadership:** the Index bio stresses a strong belief in it. [src](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/)\n- **Calls right or wrong:** nothing sourced beyond the deals themselves.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Our investment in Scope](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/): their founder screen and the industrial-AI data thesis.\n2. [Arondite raises $12M](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/): the defence-AI rationale, co-signed with Seal and Hasslinger.\n3. [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/): the operator background and full portfolio list.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. They have no public X presence linked from Index.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Scope (industrial inspection AI) | $20M (£15M), press calls it Series A | 2026-05-19 | Index led; Kurgan co-signed with Bastian Hasslinger | Susa Ventures and Entrepreneurs First were earlier backers (\"doubled down in every round\"). No A co-investors named | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) [BusinessCloud](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/) |\n| Arondite (defence AI) | Seed, $12.25M (Index about $10M) | 2025-05-01 | Index led (per Calibre); co-signed with Hannah Seal and Bastian Hasslinger | Concept Ventures led the earlier $2.25M pre-seed (2024) | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/) [Calibre](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/) |\n| Dream Games | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n| Bigger Games | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n| Birdie (healthcare) | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n| Codat (fintech) | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n| Fonoa (fintech) | not stated | not stated | not stated | | [Index team page](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Scope:** Founded by Jonny Low (CEO) and Jakob Cassiman. Index followed them for some time (\"front-row seat\") before leading the $20M round. Susa Ventures and EF were first and re-upped each round. Index was not first. [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) [BusinessCloud](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/)\n- **Arondite:** Will Blyth (CEO; ex-British Army, Palantir, Helsing) and Rob Underhill (CTO; ex-BAE Systems lead engineer, founder of Garrison Technologies). Index knew them from founding, but Concept Ventures led the 2024 pre-seed. Index then wrote about $10M of the $12.25M seed. The intro path is not stated. [Calibre](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/) [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bastian Hasslinger (Index) | Co-signer on Scope and Arondite | 2025 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/) |\n| Hannah Seal (Index) | Co-signer on Arondite | 2025 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/) |\n| King Digital Entertainment | Former COO | before 2020 | [Index](https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/) |\n| Jonny Low, Jakob Cassiman (Scope) | Portfolio founders | 2026 | [BusinessCloud](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/) |\n| Will Blyth, Rob Underhill (Arondite) | Portfolio founders | 2025 | [Calibre](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/) |\n| Susa Ventures; Entrepreneurs First | Earlier backers of Scope | before 2026-05 | [BusinessCloud](https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/) |\n| Concept Ventures | Led Arondite pre-seed before Index | 2024 | [Calibre](https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Any podcast, talk or interview, especially on King, operator-to-VC or AI in gaming? Web search was exhausted in this run, so this needs a follow-up pass.\n- Are they on the boards of Scope or Arondite, or is Hasslinger?\n- Did their King network source Dream Games or Bigger Games? Was Index a King investor? Neither is checked here.\n- Is Scope CEO Jonny Low an alumnus of the AI lab Conjecture? This is unconfirmed (flagged in the Hasslinger profile).\n- Dates of the King COO tenure. LinkedIn would have them.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.indexventures.com/team/stephane-kurgan/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/building-the-first-intelligence-layer-for-industrial-assets-our-investment-in-scope/\n- https://www.indexventures.com/perspectives/arondite-raises-12m-to-unlock-safe-scalable-human-machine-teaming-with-ai/\n- https://businesscloud.co.uk/news/15m-investment-in-uk-startup-could-end-clipboard-inspections/\n- https://www.calibredefence.co.uk/arondite-raises-12-million-in-seed-funding/\n\nBackfilled 2026-11-16: known_for.\n", "person:stephanie-zhan": "\n# Stephanie Zhan\n\n*Research note (2026-11-10): this run's web-search budget was used up before it started, so everything here comes from fetching known URLs (Sequoia pages and articles, TechCrunch, company blogs, press already in the repo). Her X announcement of the new firm returned HTTP 402, so the firm's name, size and team are still unknown. **Disambiguation:** a different investor, Brian Zhan (CRV, now Striker Venture Partners), sits on several of the same cap tables (Reflection, Skild, Ricursive). Repo notes that say \"Gazor and Zhan\", \"Zhan's same-month partnering\" or \"CRV partnered (Zhan)\" refer to Brian Zhan, not Stephanie.*\n\n## One-line take\nShe was Sequoia's seed partner on the first institutional rounds of three frontier-type labs: Skild AI (robotics foundation model, seed 2023), Reflection AI (DeepMind RL founders, seed 2024) and Ricursive Intelligence (AlphaChip founders, first round 2025). In October 2026 she left to launch her own firm ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/), [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)).\n\n## Crux\n- **Why she wins.** She commits at formation and commits fast. At Skild she met the two CMU professors on a Thursday and Sequoia was their partner by the following Tuesday ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/)). At Ricursive, Sequoia led \"their very first round from the formation\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/)). At Reflection, Sequoia calls itself the \"first and long-term partner\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/)). Each of these seeds was followed within months by a $105M-$300M round from Lightspeed and others at a 3-5x+ step-up.\n- **Her filter**, as her own deal posts put it: researcher-founders who already built the canonical system in their field (AlphaGo/Gemini RL, AlphaChip, CMU robot learning), pitched as a \"frontier AI lab\" for a category, plus a compute-bottleneck story (\"chip design is the compute bottleneck\").\n- **Constraint.** The edge was partly Sequoia's balance sheet and brand (Arc, follow-on re-ups in Skild's A, Reflection's B and Ricursive's A). Her new firm's size is unknown. If it's a concentrated fund, she can't anchor $300M+ lab rounds alone and will look more like Striker than Sequoia.\n- **What she'd never do (inferred).** Nothing in her posts suggests late entry. Every AI deal she's named on is a seed or first round. Treat this as a pattern, not a stated rule.\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Five-day seed decision on Skild.** She writes \"I first met Deepak and Abhinav on a Thursday afternoon\" and \"we were partners by Tuesday.\" This was during their initial seed raise in 2023, but the partnership was only made public in July 2024 alongside the $300M Series A ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/skild-ai/)). Sequoia then followed on as a participant in that A, which Lightspeed, Coatue, SoftBank and Bezos led ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a)).\n- **Ricursive: first round \"from the formation\", but Striker also claims inception.** Her post says Sequoia was \"leading their very first round from the formation of Ricursive Intelligence\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/), 2025-12-02). Striker's portfolio page also lists Ricursive as \"Partnered 2025, At Inception\" ([src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio)). So two firms claim the founding round, and it's unknown whether Striker was in Sequoia's $35M round or a separate one. The timing: a $35M seed at $750M (announced 2025-12-03) ([src](https://convergedigest.com/ricursive-intelligence-aims-to-accelerate-semiconductor-design/)), then a Lightspeed-led $300M A at $4B on 2026-01-26, under two months later, with Sequoia re-upping ([src](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/)).\n- **Compute thesis in her own words.** \"Compute is the most valuable resource in the AI world we live in today\" and \"chip design is the compute bottleneck\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/)). This is the AI-chips leg of THESIS.md, stated by the lead investor.\n- **Reflection: senior/junior pairing with Charlie Curnin, first money before the launch.** Sequoia's company page names both Zhan and Curnin, with Founded, Partnered and Arc all dated 2024 ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/)). The $25M seed (co-led with CRV) was only announced on 2025-03-07, the same day as the $105M A that CRV and Lightspeed co-led at about $555M ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956)). Curnin is also no longer on Sequoia's team page ([people/charlie-curnin.md](charlie-curnin.md)), which raises the question of whether Curnin joined her new firm.\n- **Robotics call made in July 2024.** \"A GPT-3 moment is coming to the world of robotics\" ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a)). Skild announced a $1.4B Series C led by SoftBank at over $14B in January 2026 ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs)).\n- **Tavus: she tried the product the night she met the founders.** Sequoia led the seed in late 2021, while the product was in alpha ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-tavus-ai-personalized-videos/)).\n- **The podcast came after the deal, not before it.** She co-hosted Sequoia's Training Data podcast with Sonya Huang, including an episode with Reflection's Misha Laskin, a founder Sequoia had already seeded ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/)). Huang used the same show to build a relationship before a deal (David Silver, then Ineffable; see [sonya-huang](sonya-huang.md)). Zhan used it to amplify a portfolio company. No sourcing-by-podcast example of hers was found.\n- **Co-investor overlap with Brian Zhan's firms.** Not a relationship claim, just shared cap tables: CRV co-led the Reflection seed with Sequoia ([src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956)), CRV was in Skild's A ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a)), and Striker claims inception at Ricursive ([src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio)).\n- Not found: check sizes, who introduced her to any founder, deals she passed on, mistakes admitted, or the new firm's name, size or LPs.\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Her named AI deals are the THESIS.md categories at the entry point THESIS.md cares about. Reflection AI is a new lab (seed 2024, now valued in the billions). Ricursive Intelligence is AI for chip design, a compute bottleneck (first round, 2025). Skild AI is a robotics foundation-model lab (seed 2023). As a newly independent manager she is both a **co-investor** (likely to be in the founding round of the next ex-DeepMind or ex-Google research team) and a **comparable** for an owner who writes ~$20M into large seeds. The key unknown is fund size: it decides whether she leads lab seeds or needs partners for them. Lower-fit parts of her record: Tavus (AI video, applied), Mach Industries (defense, with Shaun Maguire), and Linear, Middesk and Evy (non-AI SaaS, low/skip).\n\n## Background\n- Studied Computer Science at Stanford (degree year not listed). Grew up across Asia and learned to code building simple video games ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/)).\n- Joined Sequoia in 2015 ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)). Became a Partner on the seed/early team; promotion dates not found ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/)).\n- Prior jobs before Sequoia: nothing found.\n- Announced in October 2026 that she is launching her own firm. TechCrunch says she \"has meanwhile left the firm entirely\" ([src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/)). Sequoia's site still showed her page as of 2026-11-10.\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/ | Legacy Sequoia bio; still live 2026-11-10. New firm site not found |\n| Substack / blog | Sequoia \"Partnering with...\" posts (Skild, Reflection, Ricursive, Tavus) | Deal announcement posts under her byline |\n| X | https://x.com/stephzhan | Firm announcement post: https://x.com/stephzhan/status/2113303172894867859 (fetch returned 402; follower count not checked) |\n| LinkedIn | nothing found | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | Training Data (Sequoia), co-host with Sonya Huang | e.g. https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/ |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Training Data: \"Reflection AI's Misha Laskin on the AlphaGo Moment for LLMs\". Co-hosted with Sonya Huang; a portfolio founder ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/)). The page metadata date (2026-11-09) is a site artifact; original air date not confirmed.\n- Training Data: \"Jim Fan on Nvidia's Embodied AI Lab...\". Co-hosted with Sonya Huang ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-jim-fan/)). No substantive investing statements were retrieved.\n- Talks (e.g. AI Ascent): nothing retrieved.\n\n## How they think\n- **Category-defining labs from the field's original builders.** Her Ricursive goal is \"to build the frontier AI lab defining this category\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/)).\n- **Compute scarcity as thesis**, applied to chip design ([same src]).\n- **Robotics as the next \"GPT-3 moment\"**, called in July 2024 ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a)).\n- **Founders first.** Her bio stresses ambition, agency and good character, and backing companies \"from idea to IPO and beyond\" ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/)).\n- **Calls she got right.** On step-ups since her entry: Skild seed (2023) to a Series C at over $14B (Jan 2026) ([src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs)). Reflection seed (2024) to a $2B B at $8B (Oct 2025) ([src](https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence)). Ricursive at $750M to $4B in under two months ([src](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/)). Wrong calls: nothing found.\n\n## Read these first\n1. [Partnering with Ricursive Intelligence](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/) (2025-12-02): her compute and chip-design thesis, and the \"first round from formation\" claim.\n2. [Partnering with Skild](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/) (2024-07-09): the Thursday-to-Tuesday seed decision.\n3. [Partnering with Reflection](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/) (2025-03-07, with Charlie Curnin): first-partner framing.\n4. [Training Data with Misha Laskin](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/): how she talks with a lab founder.\n5. [TechCrunch on Sequoia's contraction](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/) (2026-11-04): context for her exit.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing retrieved. X was not fetchable (402).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Seed $35M at $750M (first round from formation) | 2025 (announced 2025-12-03) | Yes (Sequoia led) | None named in the announcement; Striker claims \"At Inception\" 2025 separately | [src](https://convergedigest.com/ricursive-intelligence-aims-to-accelerate-semiconductor-design/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/) |\n| Ricursive Intelligence | Series A $300M at $4B (follow-on) | 2026-01-26 | No (Lightspeed led) | DST Global, NVentures, Felicis, 49 Palms, Radical AI | [src](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/) |\n| Reflection AI | Seed $25M | Partnered 2024 (Arc 2024); announced 2025-03-07 | Co-led with CRV | CRV; Sequoia co-partner Charlie Curnin | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/) |\n| Reflection AI | Series B $2B at $8B (Sequoia re-up) | 2025-10-09 | No (Nvidia led) | Lightspeed, CRV, DST, others; her personal role in the B not stated | [src](https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence) |\n| Skild AI | Seed (amount undisclosed) | 2023 (made public 2024-07-09) | unknown | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/skild-ai/) |\n| Skild AI | Series A $300M at $1.5B (follow-on) | 2024-07-09 | No (Lightspeed, Coatue, SoftBank, Bezos led) | Felicis, Menlo, General Catalyst, CRV, SV Angel, CMU, Amazon | [src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a) |\n| Tavus | Seed | 2021 | Yes (\"to lead their seed round\") | not named | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-tavus-ai-personalized-videos/) |\n| Mach Industries | Pre-seed/seed (defense) | 2023 | unknown | Sequoia co-partner Shaun Maguire | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/), [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/) |\n| Linear | Pre-seed/seed (non-AI) | 2019 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/) |\n| Middesk | Pre-seed/seed (non-AI) | 2019 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/) |\n| Evy | Early (non-AI) | 2021 | unknown | | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Skild AI (2023).** She met Deepak Pathak and Abhinav Gupta, both CMU Robotics Institute faculty, while they were raising their initial seed. She met them on a Thursday and Sequoia was their partner by the following Tuesday. Sequoia said it would join the board. The intro path is not stated ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/)).\n- **Reflection AI (2024).** Founded, partnered and in Arc all in 2024, with Zhan and Charlie Curnin as the Sequoia partners. The $25M seed was co-led with CRV and kept quiet until the March 2025 launch ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/), [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956)). How Sequoia met Laskin and Antonoglou is not stated. Huang separately says on-air \"We backed Ioannis\" ([see sonya-huang](sonya-huang.md)).\n- **Ricursive Intelligence (2025).** Founded and partnered in 2025. Sequoia led the founding round, and Zhan is the only Sequoia partner listed ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/ricursive-intelligence/)). Felicis says it knew co-founder Azalia Mirhoseini for years through its sponsorship of her Stanford lab ([src](https://www.felicis.com/blog/ricursive-intelligence-a-step-change-on-the-path-to-agi)). Zhan's intro path is not stated.\n- **Tavus (2021).** She tried the alpha product the night after meeting Hassaan Raza and Quinn Favret, and Sequoia led the seed ([src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-tavus-ai-personalized-videos/)).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequoia Capital | Investor, then Partner on the seed/early team; left Oct 2026 | 2015-2026 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/) |\n| Charlie Curnin | Sequoia co-partner on Reflection AI | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/) |\n| Shaun Maguire | Sequoia co-partner on Mach Industries | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/) |\n| Sonya Huang | Training Data podcast co-host | unknown | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/) |\n| Misha Laskin, Ioannis Antonoglou | Seed investor (Reflection AI) | 2024 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/) |\n| Anna Goldie, Azalia Mirhoseini | Led first round (Ricursive Intelligence) | 2025 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/ricursive-intelligence/) |\n| Deepak Pathak, Abhinav Gupta | Seed investor (Skild AI); Sequoia board seat stated | 2023 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/) |\n| Hassaan Raza, Quinn Favret | Led seed (Tavus) | 2021 | [src](https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/tavus/) |\n| CRV | Seed co-lead on Reflection AI | 2024 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956) |\n| Lightspeed Venture Partners | Next-round lead after her seeds (Reflection A, Skild A, Ricursive A) | 2024-2026 | [src](https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956), [src](https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a), [src](https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/) |\n| Striker Venture Partners | Also claims inception at Ricursive (same-round status unknown) | 2025 | [src](https://www.striker.vc/portfolio) |\n\n## Open questions\n1. **New firm's name, fund size, LPs and team.** Did Charlie Curnin join? The X post is at https://x.com/stephzhan/status/2113303172894867859 and couldn't be fetched.\n2. Was Striker in Sequoia's $35M Ricursive round, or did it come in separately? Who was actually first?\n3. Did Sequoia lead Skild's 2023 seed, and how big was it? Who else was in it?\n4. How did she meet Laskin and Antonoglou, the CMU founders, and Goldie and Mirhoseini: through Arc, Stanford, or a DeepMind or Google network?\n5. Will her new firm write into Reflection, Skild or Ricursive follow-ons, and will she keep any Sequoia board seats?\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/stephanie-zhan/\n- https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/04/sequoia-hits-rewind-undoing-20-years-of-expansion/\n- https://x.com/stephzhan/status/2113303172894867859 (not fetchable, 402)\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-ricursive-intelligence-a-premier-frontier-lab-pioneering-ai-for-chip-design/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-skild/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-reflection-toward-superintelligence-with-autonomous-coding/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/partnering-with-tavus-ai-personalized-videos/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/reflection-ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/ricursive-intelligence/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/skild-ai/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/companies/tavus/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/people/shaun-maguire/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-misha-laskin/\n- https://www.sequoiacap.com/podcast/training-data-jim-fan/\n- https://siliconangle.com/?p=688956\n- https://convergedigest.com/ricursive-intelligence-aims-to-accelerate-semiconductor-design/\n- https://aijourn.com/ricursive-intelligence-raises-300-million-series-a-at-4-billion-valuation-to-accelerate-ai-driven-semiconductor-design/\n- https://www.skild.ai/blogs/announcing-our-300m-series-a\n- https://www.skild.ai/blogs\n- https://www.striker.vc/portfolio\n- https://reflection.ai/blog/building-frontier-open-intelligencence\n\nBackfilled 2026-11-16: known_for\n", "person:stephen-balaban": "\n# Stephen Balaban\n\n## One-line take\nLambda co-founder and CTO named as a Humans& seed investor. [src](https://humansand.ai/) [src](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-assembles-leadership-team-to-power-gigawatt-scale-ai-infrastructure)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh — researcher assessment: A frontier-lab seed investment alongside an operating role in AI compute infrastructure. [src](https://humansand.ai/)\n\n## Background\n- Co-founded Lambda with Michael Balaban in 2012; shifted from CEO to CTO effective May 2026. [src](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-assembles-leadership-team-to-power-gigawatt-scale-ai-infrastructure)\n- Previously the first hire at Perceptio; studied computer science and economics at the University of Michigan. Exact employment and graduation dates were not given. [src](https://crescentcove.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Crescent_Cove_Interview_Series_Balaban_v3.pdf)\n- Lambda’s 2022 Form D identifies Balaban as President/CEO and director; this is historical issuer leadership evidence, not a personal investment. [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1954966/000195496622000001/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [src](https://lambda.com/leadership) |  |\n| Substack / blog |  | Not found in checked sources |\n| X |  | Not found in checked sources |\n| LinkedIn | [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/sbalaban) |  |\n| GitHub |  | Not found in checked sources |\n| Podcasts (host) |  | Not found in checked sources |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Date not confirmed — Crescent Cove Conversations: Stephen Balaban, Co-Founder & CEO, Lambda — [src](https://crescentcove.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Crescent_Cove_Interview_Series_Balaban_v3.pdf) — Catalogued only; not mined.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Read these first\nNot written yet (skeleton page); media above is a catalogue, not an interpretive reading order.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot researched in this skeleton pass; no follower counts or inferred interaction claims.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Humans& | Seed | 2026-01-20 | No | SV Angel; Georges Harik | [src](https://humansand.ai/) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lambda | at: Co-founder and CEO | 2012 to 2026-05 | [src](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-assembles-leadership-team-to-power-gigawatt-scale-ai-infrastructure) |\n| Lambda | at: Co-founder and CTO | 2026-05 | [src](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-assembles-leadership-team-to-power-gigawatt-scale-ai-infrastructure) |\n| Perceptio | at: First hire (former) | Not found | [src](https://crescentcove.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Crescent_Cove_Interview_Series_Balaban_v3.pdf) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Individual investment check size and check-capacity segment are not disclosed; Lambda’s corporate financing is not personal angel capacity.\n- The Crescent Cove interview has no confirmed publication date in the fetched PDF; its media date is blank.\n- Personal site https://www.stephenbalaban.com/ was inaccessible. Prior-job dates and graduation year remain unconfirmed.\n- Check-capacity field is intentionally empty: no fetched source establishes an allowed capacity tier.\n- Blank affiliation dates mean unknown; historical roles are explicitly labeled former. No continuous tenure is inferred.\n\n## Sources\n- [src](https://crescentcove.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Crescent_Cove_Interview_Series_Balaban_v3.pdf)\n- [src](https://humansand.ai/)\n- [src](https://lambda.com/blog/lambda-assembles-leadership-team-to-power-gigawatt-scale-ai-infrastructure)\n- [src](https://lambda.com/leadership)\n- [src](https://www.linkedin.com/in/sbalaban)\n- [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1954966/000195496622000001/xslFormDX01/primary_doc.xml)\n", "person:stephenie-zhang": "\n# Stephenie Zhang\n\n## One-line take\na16z growth partner focused on enterprise technology and named on the firm's Exa, Temporal, and Gimlet posts. [src](https://a16z.com/author/stephenie-zhang/) [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow: Zhang's a16z remit is growth investing, although Exa and Temporal are AI-infrastructure-adjacent. [src](https://a16z.com/author/stephenie-zhang/)\n\n## Background\n- Prior to a16z, Zhang worked at Warburg Pincus on growth and late-stage technology investing. [src](https://a16z.com/author/stephenie-zhang/)\n- a16z lists her MIT computer-science degree. [src](https://a16z.com/author/stephenie-zhang/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [a16z profile](https://a16z.com/author/stephenie-zhang/) | Firm profile |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNot catalogued in this skeleton.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Read these first\nNot prioritized yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed (skeleton page).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Exa | Series C | 2026-05-20 | Yes |  | [src](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Andreessen Horowitz | Partner, Growth Investing Team |  | [src](https://a16z.com/author/stephenie-zhang/) |\n| Warburg Pincus | Former growth and late-stage technology investor |  | [src](https://a16z.com/author/stephenie-zhang/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Confirm joining and promotion dates at a16z and the individual lead partner on Exa. (checked: a16z profile and Exa post)\n\n## Sources\n- [a16z profile](https://a16z.com/author/stephenie-zhang/)\n- [Exa investment](https://a16z.com/announcement/investing-in-exa/)\n", "person:steve-bowsher": "\n# Steve Bowsher\n\n## One-line take\nHe has run In-Q-Tel's investing since 2006 and approves its deals, so his view of what agencies want explains why IQT shows up in a given AI or chip seed.\n\n## Crux\nHe is a former venture GP who now invests without a return target: \"we're not investing to try and drive financial returns\" [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel). He wins access because IQT is not a rival for ownership, and he is constrained by agency demand and by a model built around pilots, not pricing. `check_capacity` is set to sub-10m as an assumption; no cheque size was found.\n\n## Differential insight\n- IQT's scorecard: \"we measure ourselves on that pilot rate and adoption rate\" [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n- On what he found most interesting in 2024: \"pretty interesting, but I think bio is, is even more interesting\" (generative AI versus AI for biology) [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n- Sourcing: \"top-tier venture capital firms who we've had successful co-investments with\" [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow. Government strategic capital, not a co-investor peer; useful as a signal of agency demand in chips and compute seeds.\n\n## Background\nHarvard (magna cum laude); Stanford MBA; early employee at two startups; helped launch Destination E*TRADE; General Partner at InterWest Partners for eight years; at IQT led investment strategy from 2006 as Managing General Partner and EVP; now CEO [src](https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team | bio on the team page |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | |\n| LinkedIn | not checked | |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2024-09 — Tech'ed Up — Venture Capital Meets Mission Critical — https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel — transcript read; quotes above\n- NatSec Tech — Episode 86 — https://natsec-tech.simplecast.com/episodes/episode-86-steve-bowsher-on-how-in-q-tel-is-spurring-innovation — not read\n- All Quiet on the Second Front — Episode 53 — not read\n- Authority Magazine — 5 Things I Need To See Before Making A VC Investment — not read (fetch blocked)\n\n## How they think\n- Strategic, not financial: impact on agencies is the goal [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n- Pilots first: \"the heart of what we do is to, uh, facilitate the piloting of technology\" [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n\n## Read these first\n- The Tech'ed Up transcript [src](https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel).\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Palantir, Anduril, Databricks, MongoDB, Anaconda, Cloudera, Cleversafe | named in his IQT bio; rounds not stated | | no | | [src](https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNothing found.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| In-Q-Tel | CEO; led investing | 2006 | [src](https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team) |\n| InterWest Partners | former General Partner | until about 2006 | [src](https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team) |\n| Dan Gwak | his chief investment officer | | [src](https://www.iqt.org/about/our-team) |\n\n## Open questions\nDate he became CEO; which AI deals he personally sponsored.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://www.iqt.org/about/our-team ; https://www.buzzsprout.com/1851039/episodes/15688120-venture-capital-meets-mission-critical-steve-bowsher-iqt-in-q-tel\n", "person:steve-hochberg": "\n# Steve Hochberg\n\n## One-line take\nHochberg is the healthcare-structuring GP at Triatomic. The only deal his name is publicly on is Chemify (AI-driven chemistry), and SEC filings show he kept two other investing roles through the Triatomic years. Read him as a board and capital-structure operator and a door into New York healthcare capital, not as someone sourcing AI compute.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** about three decades of healthcare company-building and structured deals.\n  - A 2020 Deerfield SPAC prospectus credits him with \"over 65 transactions completed, representing approximately $1 billion in capital\" ([DFP S-1, 2020-02-21](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm)).\n  - He led the Continuum–Mount Sinai hospital merger and is still a Mount Sinai Vice Chairman.\n  - That gives him board credibility and a crossover-style feel for structure: SPACs, BDCs, structured equity.\n- **Constraint:** his attention is split. His own filed bio lists four roles running in parallel during the Triatomic years:\n  - Triatomic co-GP since 2022.\n  - Deerfield Operating Partner from 2022 to 2025.\n  - Ascent Biomedical Ventures co-founder and manager since 2004.\n  - SLR Investment Corp. independent director since 2007, re-nominated in 2026 for a term to 2029 ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n  - His network and his edge are in healthcare and medtech, not AI systems.\n- **What he'd never do (inferred):** lead an AI-application seed on research-talent grounds. His one public lead is a science-platform Series A with an existing institutional backer, which fits a structured, de-risked style.\n\n## Differential insight\n1. **\"Ex-Deerfield\" is wrong on his own SEC filing.**\n   - His 2026 SLR proxy bio reads \"Operating Partner of Deerfield Management ... from 2022 to 2025\" ([SLR DEF 14A, 2026-10-07](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n   - So he was still at Deerfield when he became a Triatomic GP (05/2022), when he led Chemify (2023-08) and when he became Managing Partner (09/2023).\n   - The Triatomic site bio gives Deerfield as 2013–2022 only ([triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai)).\n   - The same proxy still describes him as Ascent Biomedical Ventures \"Co-Founder and Manager ... since 2004\", which makes a third active investing seat.\n2. **Triatomic's hedge-fund predecessor held his SPACs before he joined.**\n   - He was President and CEO of two Deerfield SPACs.\n     - DFP Healthcare Acquisitions Corp. later traded as TOI (The Oncology Institute). The S-1 says: \"Steven Hochberg has been our President and Chief Executive Officer since our formation\" ([DFP S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm)).\n     - Deerfield Healthcare Technology Acquisitions Corp. later traded as CMAX (CareMax). He signed its 10-K certification ([10-K ex.31.1, 2021-03-04](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1813914/000110465921031739/tm2039179d1_ex31-1.htm)).\n   - Peter Zhou's Hongkou Capital, the adviser that became Triatomic, filed a Q4 2021 13F (filed 2022-02-14) showing 876,464 CareMax Class A shares (about $6.7M), 65,021 CareMax warrants and 400,000 TOI shares (about $3.9M) ([13F infotable](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266122000517/infotable.xml)).\n   - Hochberg joined Triatomic's GP LLC three months later, in 05/2022.\n   - Inference: the Zhou–Hochberg relationship probably began as Zhou backing Hochberg's de-SPACs (as a PIPE or open-market investor, not established). No filing says how they met.\n3. **He also sat on the first Deerfield SPAC.** DFB Healthcare Acquisitions Corp. (which became AdaptHealth, ticker AHCO) filed his consent as a director nominee ([DFB S-1/A exhibit list, 2018-02-12](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1725255/000104746918000757/a2234511zs-1a.htm)). That puts him on all three of Deerfield's healthcare SPACs from 2018 to 2020.\n4. **His own exposure to his longest board seat is tiny.** He has been an SLR director since 2007 but holds 2,742 shares, in the $10,001–$50,000 range ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n5. **His early-stage filter, in his own words (2011, as LSAN co-founder):** \"We are looking for unmet clinical needs.\" He described the angel network's role as \"bridging the gap between the napkin stage and the clinical testing stage\" ([NYAS, 2011-03-01](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945)).\n6. **On Chemify he took the lead personally but wasn't first money.** He said \"We are excited to be leading the series A in Chemify\" ([Univ. of Glasgow, 2023-08](https://www.gla.ac.uk/news/archiveofnews/2023/august/headline_990451_en.html)). BlueYard was already an investor. In the 2025 Series B, Triatomic is listed only as an existing backer, and the board seats went to Wing (Satpathy) and Insight (Aceves) ([Insight Partners, 2025-10-21](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/chemify-raises-more-than-50-million-in-oversubscribed-series-b-to-drive-global-expansion-of-digital-chemistry-and-discovery/)).\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The firm itself is high fit: it writes \"$10–20M initial checks\" into compute (MatX, d-Matrix, Gimlet, Efficient, Volantis, iPronics). Hochberg is personally named only on Chemify, which is AI for chemistry, not pre-training or chips, and his edge is healthcare and structured capital. He is useful to the owner as:\n- a channel into Deerfield, Mount Sinai and New York healthcare money for any AI-bio deal;\n- the person to ask about how Triatomic structures its co-invest and SPV stack.\n\nHe is not the partner to pitch a frontier lab or chip seed to. Peter Zhou (iPronics, Elegen) and Jeff Huber are better entry points for that.\n\n## Background\n- **Education:** B.S. in Business Administration from the University of Michigan; MBA from Harvard Business School ([triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai)). Age 64 per the 2026 proxy ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n- **1991:** co-founded Physicians' Online, his first company ([vcsheet](https://www.vcsheet.com/who/steve-hochberg), aggregator; the site says \"early '90s\"). He has founded or co-founded more than 30 companies per the Triatomic site, or 12 per a 2011 NYAS piece ([NYAS](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945)).\n- **2004–present:** co-founder and manager of Ascent Biomedical Ventures, an early-stage biomedical VC ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)). He also co-founded the Life Sciences Angel Network and was a NYAS Board Governor ([NYAS, 2011](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945)).\n- **2007–present:** director of SLR (Solar Capital) Investment Corp. He was also on the board of SLR Senior Investment Corp. from 2011 to April 2022 ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n- **2011–2013:** Chairman of Continuum Health Partners until its merger with Mount Sinai. He is now Vice Chairman of the Mount Sinai Health System ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n- **2013–2021:** partner in Deerfield's private transactions group, working on structured transactions ([DFP S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm); proxy).\n- **2018–2021:** Deerfield SPACs, as above. Director nominee at DFB (AdaptHealth); President and CEO of DFP (TOI) and of Deerfield Healthcare Technology Acquisitions (CareMax).\n- **2022–2025:** Deerfield Operating Partner ([SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm)).\n- **2022–present:** co-founder and co-GP of Triatomic. He has been a 25–50% member of Triatomic Management GP LLC since 05/2022 and Managing Partner since 09/2023 (Form ADV, per [firms/triatomic.md](../firms/triatomic.md)).\n- **Other boards, per an aggregator ([marketscreener](https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/STEVE-HOCHBERG-A0CRKD/), not verified):** Crosstrees Medical, Synecor, Heart Repair Technologies, Motus GI; Executive Chairman of Hearme in 30 (2022).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [triatomic.ai](https://triatomic.ai) | Team bio; no headshot (checked 2026-11-10) |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | |\n| X | nothing found | Watch for name collisions: Steve Hochberg of Elliott Wave International and Michael Hochberg (photonics founder) are different people |\n| LinkedIn | [steve-hochberg-15b1b622b](https://www.linkedin.com/in/steve-hochberg-15b1b622b/) | Linked from triatomic.ai |\n| GitHub | n/a | |\n| Podcasts (host) | none | |\n| SEC filings | [SLR Investment Corp. proxies (CIK 1418076)](https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar?action=getcompany&CIK=1418076&type=DEF+14A) | The best dated bio, refreshed every year |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2011-03 — NYAS blog Q&A, \"The Need for Entrepreneurs in Advancing Science\" (by Adam Ludwig) — [link](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945) — the LSAN filter: \"unmet clinical needs\", early-stage funding before institutions come in.\n- No podcasts or talks found from the Triatomic era (one targeted search, 2026-11-10).\n\n## How they think\n- **Clinical need first, then structure.** He funds early biomedical companies against unmet needs ([NYAS 2011](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945)), then brings in structured and public-market capital through SPACs, BDCs and roll-ups. The DFP S-1 credits him with \"rollups of companies within the services and the medtech sectors\".\n- **Platform chemistry as healthcare infrastructure.** The Chemify Series A was pitched as digital chemistry for partners \"where molecular design and manufacturing is a critical bottleneck\" (Glasgow release; second fragment was not verified as contiguous).\n- **Calls:** how the CareMax, TOI and AdaptHealth de-SPACs performed has not been checked here (open question). Pronoun note: SEC filings say \"Mr. Hochberg\" and \"his\", so this file uses \"he\".\n\n## Read these first\n1. [SLR Investment Corp. DEF 14A (2026-10-07)](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm): his own dated career summary, including the overlapping Deerfield and Ascent roles.\n2. [DFP Healthcare Acquisitions S-1 (2020-02-21)](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm): track-record numbers (65+ deals, about $1B) and how he pitches himself.\n3. [Univ. of Glasgow Chemify release (2023-08)](https://www.gla.ac.uk/news/archiveofnews/2023/august/headline_990451_en.html): his only public quote on a Triatomic deal.\n4. [NYAS 2011](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945): his early-stage filter, in his own words.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found. He has no public X, Substack or podcast presence.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Chemify | Series A $43M (£36M total) | 2023-08 | Yes (personally quoted) | Horizon Ventures, Rocketship, Possible, Alix, Eos, UK Innovation Accelerator, BlueYard (existing) | [Glasgow](https://www.gla.ac.uk/news/archiveofnews/2023/august/headline_990451_en.html) |\n| Chemify | Series B >$50M (firm-level; Hochberg not named) | 2025-10-21 | No (Wing + Insight) | 8VC, BlueYard, Rockspring, Eos | [Insight](https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/chemify-raises-more-than-50-million-in-oversubscribed-series-b-to-drive-global-expansion-of-digital-chemistry-and-discovery/) |\n| DFP Healthcare Acquisitions (became The Oncology Institute) | SPAC sponsor-side; President and CEO | 2020 | n/a | Deerfield | [S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm) |\n| Deerfield Healthcare Technology Acquisitions (became CareMax) | SPAC sponsor-side; CEO (10-K signer) | 2020–21 | n/a | Deerfield | [10-K cert.](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1813914/000110465921031739/tm2039179d1_ex31-1.htm) |\n| DFB Healthcare Acquisitions (became AdaptHealth) | Director nominee | 2018 | n/a | Deerfield | [S-1/A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1725255/000104746918000757/a2234511zs-1a.htm) |\n\n**AI and compute deals, and timing.** Chemify is his only named AI-adjacent deal. He was not first in: BlueYard was an existing investor before the Series A. No partner is named on Triatomic's compute deals (MatX, d-Matrix, Gimlet, Efficient, Chalk, Volantis), so none is attributed to him here.\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Chemify A (2023-08):** Hochberg is the quoted lead. The release describes him as \"from Triatomic Capital\", and the \"About\" section calls him \"former Deerfield Management\", which contradicts the proxy's Operating Partner 2022–2025. The intro path is unknown. Horizon Ventures (APAC) also came in on the Series A. By the Series B, Triatomic had gone from lead to existing participant without a board seat, as far as public sources show.\n- **The Hongkou–Hochberg link (inference from filings):** Hongkou held CareMax and TOI at the end of 2021. Hochberg ran both SPACs, and Triatomic took him on as an equal GP owner in 05/2022. Whether Zhou was a PIPE investor in those de-SPACs isn't established; the PIPE subscriber lists in the S-4s were not checked.\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Peter Zhou (peter-zhou) | Triatomic co-GP; Hongkou (Zhou) held Hochberg's de-SPACs before he joined | 2021 (13F) / 2022 (GP) | [13F](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266122000517/infotable.xml); ADV |\n| Jeff Huber (jeff-huber) | Triatomic co-GP; equal GP LLC member | 2022-05 | ADV via firms/triatomic.md |\n| Deerfield Management | Partner, then Operating Partner | 2013–2025 | [SLR DEF 14A](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm) |\n| Ascent Biomedical Ventures | Co-founder and manager | 2004 | same |\n| SLR Capital Partners (Michael S. Gross, Bruce Spohler) | Independent director, SLR Investment Corp. | 2007 | same |\n| Mount Sinai Health System | Vice Chairman (ex-Continuum chair) | 2013 | same |\n| Lee Cronin / Chemify | Led the Series A | 2023-08 | [Glasgow](https://www.gla.ac.uk/news/archiveofnews/2023/august/headline_990451_en.html) |\n| Life Sciences Angel Network | Co-founder | before 2011 | [NYAS](https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Was Hongkou or Zhou a PIPE investor in the CareMax or TOI de-SPACs? Check the S-4 subscription agreements (DHTA–CareMax 2021; DFP–TOI 2021).\n- Did he really leave Deerfield in 2025, and does Ascent Biomedical Ventures still invest? Ascent's activity has not been verified.\n- Does he hold a Chemify board or observer seat?\n- Which Triatomic deals besides Chemify did he source? Edison Scientific (AI for science) and Elegen are possible candidates given his bio background, but nothing is sourced.\n- Photo: no permitted source. triatomic.ai has no headshots, and the Deerfield team page (deerfield.com/team/steven-hochberg) now returns 404.\n\n## Sources\n- https://triatomic.ai (bio; fetched 2026-11-10)\n- SLR Investment Corp. DEF 14A, 2026-10-07: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1418076/000119312526416961/d103699ddef14a.htm\n- DFP Healthcare Acquisitions S-1, 2020-02-21: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1799191/000104746920000999/a2240771zs-1.htm\n- Deerfield Healthcare Technology Acquisitions 10-K ex.31.1, 2021-03-04: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1813914/000110465921031739/tm2039179d1_ex31-1.htm\n- DFB Healthcare Acquisitions S-1/A, 2018-02-12: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1725255/000104746918000757/a2234511zs-1a.htm\n- Hongkou Capital 13F infotable (Q4 2021): https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1852813/000117266122000517/infotable.xml\n- Univ. of Glasgow Chemify Series A: https://www.gla.ac.uk/news/archiveofnews/2023/august/headline_990451_en.html\n- Insight Partners Chemify Series B: https://www.insightpartners.com/ideas/chemify-raises-more-than-50-million-in-oversubscribed-series-b-to-drive-global-expansion-of-digital-chemistry-and-discovery/\n- NYAS 2011: https://www.nyas.org/?p=23945\n- Aggregators (secondary): https://www.vcsheet.com/who/steve-hochberg ; https://ca.marketscreener.com/insider/STEVE-HOCHBERG-A0CRKD/\n", "person:steve-jang": "\n# Steve Jang\n\n*Created 2026-11-11 from his own words: about 55 public items catalogued in `data/rows/steve-jang-voice-media.csv`, 7 of them read in full. Sources do not state his pronouns, so this file uses \"they\". YouTube quotes come from auto-captions, so names in them are sometimes garbled (\"Kanye\" for Kanyi, \"a lad Gill\" for Elad Gil).*\n\n## One-line take\nAn operator-turned-seed-lead with ten $1B+ companies who now leads AI infra, generative-media and robotics rounds of up to $100M. They source from alumni networks (ex-Uber, ex-Coinbase), introduce portfolio companies to frontier labs early, and quietly back small emerging funds as an LP.\n\n## Crux\n- **Why they win:** relationship-led sourcing and patience. They tracked Humane's founders for months through ex-Apple friends before getting a meeting [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg). About 20% of Kindred's portfolio founders were ex-Uber or ex-Coinbase in 2023 [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg). After investing they work hands-on, then introduce the company to the right partner (Humane to Sam Altman while OpenAI was a nonprofit).\n- **What constrains them:** Kindred has only two partners. They don't do biotech at seed (\"biotechnology aside which we don't invest in\") [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg). LPs gave little weight to their angel record when Kindred raised Fund I [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- **What they'd never do:** fix a check size by stage (\"The idea of fixing a set amount to a stage\") [src](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures), or skip a sector just because it is unfamiliar (\"the most boring thing I can think of\") [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g).\n\n## Differential insight\n- **Humane origin (2019).** Two close ex-Apple friends tipped them off. They tracked the founders for months, then \"finally I got an introduction from one of my friends.\" They cut a Kindred offsite short to take Kanyi Maqubela to the demo. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (49:56)\n- **Lab introduction.** Kindred introduced Humane to Sam Altman \"at that time open AI was very much a nonprofit research organization.\" Jang says Altman later invested personally. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (51:25)\n- **They back small funds as an LP.** Jang is an LP in Scalar Capital (Linda Xie), Paradigm (Matt Huang, Fred Ehrsam) and Rackhouse (Kevin Novak, ex-Uber head of ML): \"it's not sort of a highly calculated Financial exercise.\" In 2023 they also backed a former intern's small AI fund (manager not named). [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (34:33, 40:08)\n- **Who helped raise Fund I.** Rob Hayes and Josh Kopelman \"introduced us to a lot of LPS gave us a lot of advice.\" Chris Dixon and Marc Andreessen also helped, and Jang calls Andreessen \"a great LP\" of Kindred (auto-caption source, medium confidence). [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (44:02, 1:06:01)\n- **Follow-on behaviour.** For conviction bets they lead the seed, then invest super pro-rata \"or even co-leading follow on rounds.\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (52:25)\n- **Uber was a hard seed raise.** \"there were several people who I won't name but uh declined.\" Travis Kalanick was still only an angel when Jang asked him about joining full-time. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g) (14:54, 15:23; recorded July 2020)\n- **Timing as a fourth pillar.** Fred Wilson weighs market, product and team; Jang adds \"the fourth thing that um I offered was that timing is super important.\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g) (25:03)\n- **Admitted mistake.** \"I started reinvesting everything I had into other people's startups, which I don't recommend.\" [src](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures) (2021)\n- **Diligence by reading code.** \"You can go into their github or API documentation.\" They prefer a demo to a deck. [src](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures)\n- **Blue Bottle without a deck.** True Ventures made the introduction, and Jang told the founders \"send it to me but I'm in.\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) (1:01:18)\n- **Coinbase.** Kindred invested in 2014 and bought bitcoin at the same time; \"it was a non-consensus industry sector.\" They sold nothing at the 2021 direct listing. [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KnWFhrhXlhE) (1:18)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**High.** Jang personally leads Kindred's frontier-AI deals: fal's Series A (and through the D), PlayAI's $21M seed (board seat; Meta acquired it in 2025), Causal Labs, Sophont, Overworld, Kotoba, Hydra Host's $100M A, Parasail's $32M A (co-lead) and Dream Labs' $90M seed (board seat). See the Portfolio table below. The LP side also fits: Jang personally backs small, concentrated managers they have worked with, such as Kevin Novak's Rackhouse. That makes them a plausible LP or co-investor for a concentrated AI fund. One caution is that most Kindred checks are still seed-sized.\n\n## Background\n- Studied English literature at UC Berkeley and built websites as an undergraduate in the 1990s [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- Co-founder or founding team member at imeem (acquired by MySpace), SoundTracking (acquired by Rhapsody) and Bitski (acquired by Phantom) [src](https://kindredventures.com/about/steve-jang/).\n- Adviser to and angel in Uber from its founding in 2009 [src](https://kindredventures.com/about/steve-jang/). Made about 50 angel investments of $25K-$250K (Coinbase, Blue Bottle, Postmates, Zymergen) [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-kanyi-maqubela-form-or-fund-as-kindred-ventures/).\n- Started Kindred in 2014 as a solo angel vehicle [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ut0SrT2tfrs). Formalised it with Kanyi Maqubela as Fund I in 2019 [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1765533/000176553319000002/primary_doc.xml). On the Forbes Midas List in 2023 and 2024 [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-named-to-2024-midas-list/).\n- Family is originally from Korea with roots in Japan. They helped Uber, Coinbase and Blue Bottle expand into Korea and Japan [src](https://tim.blog/2023/12/01/steve-jang-live-from-south-korea-transcript/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://kindredventures.com/about/steve-jang/ | Bio; deal memos with founder Q&As at /announcement |\n| Substack / blog | nothing found | Writes through Kindred announcements and research |\n| X | https://x.com/stevejang | Handle per Kindred firm file; follower count not checked |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/stevejang1/ | Listed on TWiT profile |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | nothing found | Guest only |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nThe full list (55 items, 2017-2026) is in `data/rows/steve-jang-voice-media.csv`. The ones that matter most:\n- 2017-03 — Slush Tokyo 2017 panel with Aaron Batalion and Josh Felser — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e0Q9GzQzBhE) — their angel thesis then: consumer hardware only with a subscription model; in Japan and Korea the Series A gap was their main worry.\n- 2020-08 — Village Global Venture Stories (Erik Torenberg) — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g) — Uber's side-project origin, the timing pillar, the \"rabbit hole\" approach.\n- 2021-03 — Mercury Series Tea (with Kanyi Maqubela) — [link](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures) — lead checks, concentration, no fixed check size, reading GitHub during diligence.\n- 2021-04 — CNBC on the Coinbase listing — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KnWFhrhXlhE) — invested in 2014 and did not sell.\n- 2023-10 — Smart Venture / Venture with Grace #140 — [link](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) — Humane, the OpenAI introduction, their LP positions, Fund I LP intros. The richest single source.\n- 2023-11 — Tim Ferriss #707, live from Seoul — [transcript](https://tim.blog/2023/12/01/steve-jang-live-from-south-korea-transcript/) — Korean culture; Korean VC began as a government-backed media industry.\n- 2024-03 — The Dave Chang Show, \"So You Wanna Be a Seed-Stage Investor?\" — [link](https://www.theringer.com/podcasts/the-dave-chang-show/2024/03/04/so-you-wanna-be-a-seed-stage-investor-with-steve-jang) — not mined.\n- 2025-2026 — Bloomberg, CNBC, NYSE, Upfront Summit (Aravind Srinivas in 2025, Burkay Gur in 2026), HumanX, Startup Grind with Nuro — not mined.\n\n## How they think\n- **Timing is the fourth pillar,** alongside market, product and team [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g).\n- **Non-consensus \"moonshots\"** that open a new category beat crowded lanes like marketplaces and SaaS (\"investing in companies that seem very very non-consensus\") [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- **Emotion matters at seed:** before a product exists, \"it has to be emotional emotional investing\" on top of the analysis [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g).\n- **Open-source models (2023):** closed models are the best platform today, but open models allow \"that many flowers blooming type of variation\" [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- **Calls that worked:** Uber (2009), Coinbase (2014), Humane's early AI-device thesis (2019; the product later failed and HP bought its assets), and fal (Series A at $80M, through the Series D).\n\n## Read these first\n- [Smart Venture #140 (2023)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg): the Humane deal, LP positions, Fund I raise, advisers.\n- [Mercury Series Tea (2021)](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures): a human-edited transcript of how Kindred decides and sizes checks.\n- [Village Global (2020)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g): Uber's origin, timing, beginner's mind.\n- [TechCrunch on Kindred's formation model (2019)](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-kanyi-maqubela-form-or-fund-as-kindred-ventures/): formation plus seed; target 10-20% by the Series A.\n- [Kindred's $355M funds post (2026)](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/kindred-ventures-announces-our-new-355m-set-of-funds/): the current AI thesis.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot analysed this run (X was not fetched). Their public voice runs mostly through Kindred announcements, TV hits (CNBC, Bloomberg, Fox Business, NYSE) and conference stages.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Uber | Seed / angel (adviser) | 2009-2010 | no | First Round (Rob Hayes) | [src](https://kindredventures.com/about/steve-jang/) |\n| Coinbase | Early (angel/Kindred) | 2014 | unknown | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KnWFhrhXlhE) |\n| Blue Bottle Coffee | Angel | before 2014 | no | True Ventures | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Humane | Seed (Kindred-led) | 2019 | yes | Sam Altman later | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| PlayAI | Seed ($21M combined) | 2024-06 | yes | Race Capital; 500 Global; YC | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/playai-x-meta-superintelligence-lab/) |\n| Aww | Seed ($6M) | 2024-07 | yes | | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/our-investment-in-aww-a-virtual-human-company/) |\n| fal | Series A ($14M) | 2024-09 | yes | a16z; First Round | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/our-investment-in-fal-ai-inference-platform-for-generative-media/) |\n| Causal Labs | Seed ($6M) | 2025-03 | yes | | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/causal-labs-pioneering-ai-driven-physics-models-to-address-causal-intelligence/) |\n| Sophont | Seed ($9.22M combined) | 2025-09 | yes | Jeff Dean; Upfront | [src](https://sophont.med/blog/seed_announcement) |\n| Scorecard | Seed ($3.75M) | 2025-09 | yes | | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/scorecard-solving-ai-agent-evaluation-at-scale/) |\n| Dedalus Labs | Seed ($11M) | 2025-10 | co-led | | [src](https://pubt.io/view/04FE6C24A787599A474AEFD975A3234B8E3FD6FA) |\n| Overworld | Pre-seed ($4.5M) | 2026-01 | yes | | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/overworld-building-real-time-world-models/) |\n| Parasail | Series A ($32M) | 2026-04 | co-led | | [src](https://www.vcaonline.com/news/2026041505/parasail-raises-32m-series-a-to-build-the-supercloud-that-puts-developers-in-control-of-their-ai/) |\n| Hydra Host | Series A ($100M) | 2026-06 | yes | NVIDIA; ARK; Magnetar | [src](https://hydrahost.com/blog/news/hydra-host-raises-100m-series-a/) |\n| Kotoba | Seed ($10M) | 2026-06 | yes | | [src](https://gamesbeat.com/kotoba-technologies-raises-10m-for-real-time-voice-ai-platform-in-east-asia/) |\n| Dream Labs Robotics | Seed ($90M) | 2026-10 | no (board) | B Capital (lead); SV Angel; Thomas Wolf | [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/dream-labs-building-the-brain-for-everyday-robots/) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **Uber (2009):** Garrett Camp's side project. Jang wrote Camp a check [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-kanyi-maqubela-form-or-fund-as-kindred-ventures/) and, as an adviser, helped get a contractor-built iOS/Android prototype made. Several seed investors passed [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g).\n- **Humane (2019):** months of tracking through ex-Apple friends, a friend's introduction, a demo that made them cut an offsite short, a Kindred-led seed, then an introduction to Sam Altman at OpenAI [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- **Blue Bottle:** Jang was a daily customer at the Hayes Valley garage. True Ventures introduced the founders, and Jang committed before seeing a deck, then helped with the Japan and Korea launches [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg).\n- **Bitski:** started as Riff, a video app. Kindred helped pivot it into crypto developer tooling [src](https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-kanyi-maqubela-form-or-fund-as-kindred-ventures/).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kanyi Maqubela | Co-founder and MP at Kindred; co-investor before Kindred while at Collaborative Fund | before 2019 | [src](https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures) |\n| Garrett Camp | Close friend; Jang advised Uber from its start | 2009 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g) |\n| Rob Hayes / Josh Kopelman (First Round) | Introduced LPs to Kindred Fund I | 2018-19 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Marc Andreessen | Kindred LP per Jang (auto-caption) | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Om Malik / Jon Callaghan (True Ventures) | Investors and board members in Jang's startup; advisers | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Scalar Capital; Paradigm; Rackhouse | Jang is an LP | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Sam Altman | Jang introduced Humane to him | 2019-20 | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg) |\n| Erik Torenberg (Village Global) | Frequent co-investor | | [src](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Who is the former intern whose small AI-focused fund Jang backed in 2023? (He worked at Upstart and then a UK firm.)\n- Is Marc Andreessen an LP in a Kindred fund, and which one? The only source so far is an auto-caption.\n- Not yet mined: The Dave Chang Show (2024), the Web3 Gen Z podcast (mirror.xyz returned 403), Generative Now (2025), the 2025-2026 conference talks (Upfront with Srinivas, HumanX, the Josh Constine fireside) and their X posts.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tczuyh3dtSg (Smart Venture #140, 2023-10-05)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ueKe7Vy_-7g (Village Global, recorded July 2020)\n- https://mercury.com/blog/kindred-ventures (Series Tea, 2021-03-29)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e0Q9GzQzBhE (Slush Tokyo 2017, uploaded 2018)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KnWFhrhXlhE (CNBC, 2021-04-14)\n- https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ut0SrT2tfrs (Forbes, 2023-06-09)\n- https://tim.blog/2023/12/01/steve-jang-live-from-south-korea-transcript/\n- https://kindredventures.com/about/steve-jang/\n- https://kindredventures.com/announcement/steve-jang-kanyi-maqubela-form-or-fund-as-kindred-ventures/\n- https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1765533/000176553319000002/primary_doc.xml\n- `data/deals.csv` and `data/rounds.csv` rows for steve-jang\n", "person:steve-jurvetson": "\n# Steve Jurvetson\n\n## One-line take\nFuture Ventures co-founder focused on founder-led, mission-driven companies at the edge of new industry formation. [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh — documented early investments in SpaceX, Tesla, and Planet Labs plus a technical hardware background. [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/)\n\n## Background\n- Jurvetson co-founded Future Ventures and Draper Fisher Jurvetson. [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/)\n- Before venture, he was an R&D engineer at Hewlett-Packard and held roles at Apple, NeXT, and Bain & Company. [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/)\n- He earned undergraduate and graduate electrical-engineering and business degrees at Stanford. [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [Future Ventures](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/) |  |\n| Substack / blog | [Blog](https://steve.blog) |  |\n| X | [@FutureJurvetson](https://x.com/FutureJurvetson) |  |\n| LinkedIn | [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/in/stevejurvetson) |  |\n| GitHub |  | Not catalogued in this skeleton pass |\n| Podcasts (host) |  | Not catalogued in this skeleton pass |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNot catalogued in this skeleton pass.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Read these first\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Social engagement\nNot researched in this skeleton pass.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SpaceX | Early investment |  |  |  | [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/) |\n| Tesla | Early investment |  |  |  | [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/) |\n| Planet Labs | Early investment |  |  |  | [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Future Ventures | at: Co-founder |  | [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/) |\n| Draper Fisher Jurvetson | at: Co-founder (former) |  | [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/) |\n| Hewlett-Packard | at: R&D engineer (former) |  | [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Exact dates for career transitions and current person-attributed round records. (checked: [Future Ventures profile](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/))\n\n## Sources\n- [src](https://steve-jurvetson.squarespace.com/)\n- [src](https://x.com/FutureJurvetson)\n", "person:steve-loughlin": "\n# Steve Loughlin\n\n## One-line take\nLoughlin is an Accel early-stage partner and former RelateIQ founder. His book is mostly enterprise SaaS and security, but he co-led both the seed and the A of Bob McGrew's Arda. That frontier-adjacent deal came from a relationship of more than twenty years, not from a research-world position. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda)\n\n## Crux\n- **Why he wins:** he is a founder-turned-partner, and founders seek him out. Jason Boehmig went looking for him because people called RelateIQ their favourite place to work. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/)\n- **His network:** he keeps backing people he has already worked with or known for years. He wrote the first check into Airkit, started by his RelateIQ COO and CTO [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit). He incubated Clockwise, started by RelateIQ platform engineers [src](https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise). He had known the Vorlon team for years [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vorlon-detect-and-remediate-api-security-threats), and McGrew for 20+ years.\n- **How he decides:** he looks for founders who were the customer for the problem they are solving, and then tries hard to break the thesis before committing (AssemblyAI). [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai)\n- **Constraint:** his centre of gravity is horizontal enterprise software (sales tooling, data reliability, security). Research-lab deals reach him through his personal network, not through a sourcing engine.\n\n## Differential insight\n- AssemblyAI nearly died at the paper screen. He had Covid, and the deck looked like \"another voice-to-text company\". He forwarded it to Sara Ittelson, about two weeks into her partnership, and she pulled him back in. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) (12:34)\n- His diligence ritual: after meeting Dylan Fox, the two partners called him daily for six to ten days, saying \"we got to find something wrong with this\". [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) (14:07)\n- He framed the AssemblyAI A as a pre-ChatGPT contrarian bet: \"an AI company at that point was non-obvious\". [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) (16:03)\n- The Ironclad A turned on a slide the founder underrated: each new legal user added about nine users from other departments. Loughlin's words: \"for every legal user Ironclad added, they added nine other users\". He signed the deal from the hospital on the day his child was born. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/)\n- Before ChatGPT, AI came up at every Ironclad board meeting: \"every board meeting we talked about should we do something with AI\". [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt) (06:21)\n- He says generative AI makes repricing away from seats \"not just aspirational, it's also existential\" for software companies. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt) (20:43)\n- He met Barr Moses in 2018, before she had chosen an idea, and told her to go for it. Her recollection: \"this investor believes in me even more than I believe in myself\". [src](https://www.accel.com/news/secrets-to-scaling-with-monte-carlos-barr-moses)\n- Hidden network link: as a Stanford GSB classmate, he introduced Jared Weinstein (later Thrive) to Joe Lonsdale and Shyam Sankar, and Weinstein went on to Palantir. Weinstein: \"a guy named Steve Laughlin, who is now an Accel partner\". [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) (1:11:36)\n- Arda already had Anduril as a customer at its stealth launch: \"Anduril Industries already relies on Arda’s technology\". [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda)\n- The CHAOS Industries post he wrote announces \"Accel’s $145 million investment in\" a defense company founded in 2022. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-chaos-bringing-groundbreaking-new-technologies-to-national-defense)\n\n## In their own words (2026-11-10)\nMined from two Spotlight On transcripts he hosted (2023), the TechCrunch Extra Crunch Live recap (2021) and 13 Accel memos he wrote (2016–2026). The audio-only SaaStr (2017) and DealMakers (2019) episodes are not yet transcribed.\n- **First-check identity:** in 2016 he wrote that he wants to be there at the moment a founder sets an audacious goal, with an AI/ML-plus-data thesis from day one. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/relateiq-founder-steve-loughlin-on-joining-accel-as-an-investor)\n- **Founder-market rule:** \"The bar is you, you would've been a customer.\" He treats customer meetings as a weak proxy for understanding the problem. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) (18:00)\n- **Lived-pain deals:** his Scratchpad, Vivun, Clockwise and Airkit theses each start from a pain he had as RelateIQ CEO. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/accels-seed-in-scratchpad-the-first-workspace-for-revenue-teams) [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vivun-the-worlds-first-platform-for-presales)\n- **Board style:** he agrees the plan with the founder up front so he can push against it. With Boehmig, a weekly meeting the founder could cancel became biweekly, then async. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/)\n- **AI cycle view (Oct 2023):** \"we're definitely in a hype cycle\". He expects an App Store-style burst of experiments with a few durable winners. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) (29:31, 34:21)\n- **Platform shifts:** \"the biggest mistake people can make is not engaging in it\". [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt) (28:32)\n- **Seed-then-A habit:** Stairwell (met Nov 2019, seed 2020, A 2021), Monte Carlo (seed and A), Vorlon (seed 2022, A 2024), Clockwise (incubated, seed, A co-lead with Greylock), Arda (seed and A together). [src](https://www.accel.com/news/an-inside-out-approach-to-security-threats-our-continued-investment-in-stairwell) [src](https://www.accel.com/news/launching-the-data-reliability-category-with-monte-carlo)\n\n## Fit with my lens\n**Medium.** The deals that matter for the thesis:\n- **Arda:** co-led the seed and A of a lab-adjacent company founded by McGrew, ex-OpenAI chief research officer. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda) The seed was reported at about $70M on a $700M valuation (WSJ, via secondary sources in `data/rounds.csv`).\n- **AssemblyAI:** speech foundation models, Series A and C lead. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-continued-investment-in-assemblyai)\n- **CHAOS Industries:** defense hardware with AI. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-chaos-bringing-groundbreaking-new-technologies-to-national-defense)\n\nThe rest of the book is enterprise SaaS and security: Ironclad, Monte Carlo, Vivun, Scratchpad, Productiv, Stairwell, Vorlon. He is a useful co-lead on big seeds for ex-lab founders he knows personally, but he does not originate frontier-research deals. The McGrew/Palantir/Stanford network is the reason to know him.\n\n## Background\n- Grew up in Portland, Oregon; Stanford graduate. [src](https://www.accel.com/team/steve-loughlin) Stanford GSB classmate of Jared Weinstein. [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein)\n- President and CEO of Affinity Circles; advised or invested in Palantir, Addepar and Roam Analytics. [src](https://cloud.substack.com/p/saastr-130-accels-steve-loughlin-142)\n- Brief entrepreneur-in-residence at Morgenthaler Ventures in early 2011. [src](https://venturebeat.com/ai/relateiq-thinks-it-can-beat-salesforce-with-better-big-data-and-40m)\n- Co-founded RelateIQ with Adam Evans in July 2011. Salesforce bought it in 2014 for close to $400M. [src](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SalesforceIQ) Accel led RelateIQ's Series A. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit)\n- Stayed inside Salesforce after the deal (\"RelateIQ and then Salesforce\"). [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vivun-the-worlds-first-platform-for-presales)\n- Joined Accel as a Partner in September 2016. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/relateiq-founder-steve-loughlin-on-joining-accel-as-an-investor)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | https://www.accel.com/team/steve-loughlin | Accel team page; 18-19 portfolio logos (2026-11) |\n| Substack / blog | https://www.accel.com/news | Accel posts he authored, about 1-2 a year (16 found, 2016–2026) |\n| X | nothing found | No verified handle linked from Accel or press |\n| LinkedIn | https://www.linkedin.com/in/steveloughlin/ | Linked from the Accel team page |\n| GitHub | nothing found | |\n| Podcasts (host) | https://www.accel.com/news/podcasts | Hosted 3 Spotlight On episodes (AssemblyAI, Ironclad, Monte Carlo), 2023-2024 |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- 2017-06 — SaaStr — Ep. 130, founding RelateIQ, Benioff lessons, market choice — https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/saastr-130-accels-steve-loughlin-on-founding-relateiq/id1089973241?i=1000386727793 — no transcript found; not mined\n- 2019-09 — DealMakers (Alejandro Cremades) — selling RelateIQ, investing in AI from a billion-dollar fund — https://www.audioscrape.com/podcast/dealmakers/episode/steve-loughlin-on-selling-his-business-to-salesforce-for-390m-and-now-investing-from-a-billion-dollar-fund-in-ai-startups-15429 — not mined\n- 2021-02 — TechCrunch Extra Crunch Live, with Jason Boehmig — https://techcrunch.com/video/steve-loughlin-accel-jason-boehmig-ironclad/ — Ironclad A origin, the nine-users slide, board cadence, pricing\n- 2023-09 — Startup Grind, with Barr Moses, on building a category with customers — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_BILhYiM57g — not mined (captions not retrievable)\n- 2023-10 — Accel Spotlight On S1E1, AssemblyAI (co-host with Sara Ittelson) — https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai — how the deal nearly got missed; hype-cycle view\n- 2023-12 — Accel Spotlight On S1E8, Ironclad's Cai GoGwilt — https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt — AI on every board agenda; pricing is existential\n- 2024-05 — Accel Spotlight On S2E9, Monte Carlo's Barr Moses — https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/monte-carlos-barr-moses-on-creating-a-playbook-for/id1710032122?i=1000656541603 — no transcript; not mined\n\n## How they think\n- Main theses: data, AI/ML and new interfaces will change how work gets done (2016). [src](https://www.accel.com/news/relateiq-founder-steve-loughlin-on-joining-accel-as-an-investor) He looks for horizontal \"core object\" collaboration plays (Ironclad's contract) and observability-style categories (Monte Carlo as \"APM for data\"). [src](https://www.accel.com/news/launching-the-data-reliability-category-with-monte-carlo)\n- Contrarian views: an AI company was a non-obvious bet in 2022. Incumbents with data and workflow can win generative AI if they jump in. Seat pricing is dying. Fear of unintended consequences should not slow innovation. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt)\n- Calls right: AssemblyAI before ChatGPT; Monte Carlo pre-company (2018); Ironclad as horizontal software rather than legal tech. Calls wrong or open: Airkit, Clockwise and Scratchpad have not visibly broken out; nothing found on admitted mistakes.\n\n## Read these first\n- [Spotlight On: AssemblyAI transcript](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai): how a deal he nearly passed on was won, and his diligence ritual.\n- [Spotlight On: Ironclad transcript](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt): his view of generative AI as a board-level and pricing issue.\n- [TechCrunch ECL recap](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/): the Ironclad A origin and his board mechanics.\n- [Our seed in Airkit](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit): his habit of re-backing his own team, in his own words.\n- [Our investment in Arda](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda): the McGrew relationship and the Anduril link.\n\n## Social engagement\nNothing found: no verified X account. His public output is Accel portfolio posts and portfolio-founder podcasts.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Arda | Seed + Series A | 2026 | co-led | co-lead not named in Loughlin's post; Founders Fund reported on the seed (WSJ via data/rounds.csv) | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda) |\n| CHAOS Industries | Accel $145M investment | 2024-11 | not stated | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-chaos-bringing-groundbreaking-new-technologies-to-national-defense) |\n| Vorlon | Seed; Series A | 2022; 2024-04 | A announced by Accel | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vorlon-detect-and-remediate-api-security-threats) |\n| AssemblyAI | Series A; Series C | 2022; 2023-11 | yes (both) | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-continued-investment-in-assemblyai) |\n| Stairwell | Seed; Series A | 2020; 2021-09 | led seed; co-led A | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/an-inside-out-approach-to-security-threats-our-continued-investment-in-stairwell) |\n| Scratchpad | Seed | 2020 | yes | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/accels-seed-in-scratchpad-the-first-workspace-for-revenue-teams) |\n| Vivun | Series A; follow-on | 2020-10; 2021-02 | led A | Menlo, Salesforce Ventures, Atlassian Ventures (follow-on) | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-continued-partnership-with-vivun-empowering-presales) |\n| Airkit | Seed (first check) | 2020-10 announced | yes | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit) |\n| Monte Carlo | Seed; Series A | by 2020-09 | yes (both) | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/launching-the-data-reliability-category-with-monte-carlo) |\n| Clockwise | Incubation; Seed; Series A | by 2019-06 | led seed; co-led A | Greylock (John Lilly) | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise) |\n| Productiv | Series A | 2019-04 | yes | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/congratulations-productiv-pioneering-the-concept-of-application-engagement) |\n| Ironclad | Series A ($8M) | 2017 | yes; board | | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/) |\n| Also on Accel page: Ascend.io, Centaur Labs, Nines, Poggio, Split, VoiceOps | | | | | [src](https://www.accel.com/team/steve-loughlin) |\n\n## Deal stories\n- **AssemblyAI (2022):** the deck arrived while he had Covid and looked generic. He handed it to newly arrived partner Sara Ittelson, who was excited. He met Dylan Fox a day or two later, and the two partners talked to Fox daily for 6-10 days trying to find a flaw before leading the A. Accel led the C in 2023. [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai)\n- **Ironclad (2017):** Boehmig reached him through a trusted reference. Loughlin and Vas Natarajan expected a legal-tech pitch. The \"contract as horizontal collaboration object\" reframe and the nine-users slide converted him, and he led the $8M A and signed from the hospital. [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/) [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt)\n- **Monte Carlo (2018-2020):** he met Barr Moses while she was still interviewing about 50 founders, before she had chosen an idea, and encouraged her to start. Accel led the seed and A. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/secrets-to-scaling-with-monte-carlos-barr-moses)\n- **Airkit (2020):** his RelateIQ COO Steve Ehikian and CTO Adam Evans pitched him, and he asked to write the first check. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit)\n- **Clockwise (2019):** its founders were RelateIQ core-platform engineers. Accel incubated it, led the seed and co-led the A with Greylock. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise)\n- **Arda (2026):** he has known Bob McGrew for 20+ years, and Anduril was already a user at launch. Accel co-led the seed and A together, $230M in total. [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bob McGrew | known 20+ years; co-led Arda seed and A | by 2006 (\"more than two decades\" as of 2026) | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda) |\n| Adam Evans | RelateIQ co-founder; Airkit first check | 2011 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit) |\n| Steve Ehikian | RelateIQ COO; Airkit first check | | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit) |\n| Jared Weinstein | Stanford GSB classmate; introduced him to Lonsdale and Sankar | Stanford years | [src](https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein) |\n| Sara Ittelson | Accel partner; AssemblyAI co-sourcing | 2022 | [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai) |\n| Vas Natarajan | Accel partner; Ironclad first meeting | 2017 | [src](https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt) |\n| Jason Boehmig | Ironclad CEO; board seat since Series A | 2017 | [src](https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/) |\n| John Lilly (Greylock) | Clockwise Series A co-lead | 2019 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise) |\n| Kendall Collins | ex-Salesforce exec who advised the RelateIQ integration; brought in as Accel EIR | 2019 | [src](https://www.accel.com/news/welcome-kendall-collins-as-accel-executive-in-residence) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Transcribe SaaStr 130 (2017) and DealMakers (2019): these are the only long-form solo interviews and likely hold RelateIQ fundraising stories, the Benioff relationship and early Accel check sizes.\n- How does he know McGrew? Both were at Stanford around the same time, and Loughlin advised Palantir, where McGrew worked. The link is unconfirmed.\n- Who were the 2026 Arda seed co-lead and the A co-lead? Founders Fund and Khosla are reported in existing rows.\n- The lead `steve-laughlin` (needs-id, surfaced by jared-weinstein) is this person; it should be merged as an alias.\n- No admitted mistakes or passes were found in his public words.\n\n## Sources\n- https://www.accel.com/team/steve-loughlin\n- https://www.accel.com/news/relateiq-founder-steve-loughlin-on-joining-accel-as-an-investor\n- https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/assemblyai\n- https://www.accel.com/podcast-episodes/ironclad-cai-gogwilt\n- https://techcrunch.com/2021/02/19/ironclads-jason-boehmig-the-objective-of-pricing-is-to-become-less-wrong-over-time/\n- https://techcrunch.com/video/steve-loughlin-accel-jason-boehmig-ironclad/\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-arda\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-investment-in-chaos-bringing-groundbreaking-new-technologies-to-national-defense\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vorlon-detect-and-remediate-api-security-threats\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-continued-investment-in-assemblyai\n- https://www.accel.com/news/an-inside-out-approach-to-security-threats-our-continued-investment-in-stairwell\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-continued-partnership-with-vivun-empowering-presales\n- https://www.accel.com/news/accels-seed-in-scratchpad-the-first-workspace-for-revenue-teams\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-series-a-in-vivun-the-worlds-first-platform-for-presales\n- https://www.accel.com/news/our-seed-in-airkit\n- https://www.accel.com/news/launching-the-data-reliability-category-with-monte-carlo\n- https://www.accel.com/news/delivering-time-with-clockwise\n- https://www.accel.com/news/congratulations-productiv-pioneering-the-concept-of-application-engagement\n- https://www.accel.com/news/welcome-kendall-collins-as-accel-executive-in-residence\n- https://www.accel.com/news/secrets-to-scaling-with-monte-carlos-barr-moses\n- https://www.jacksondahl.com/dialectic/jared-weinstein\n- https://cloud.substack.com/p/saastr-130-accels-steve-loughlin-142\n- https://venturebeat.com/ai/relateiq-thinks-it-can-beat-salesforce-with-better-big-data-and-40m\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SalesforceIQ\n", "person:steve-papa": "\n# Steve Papa\n\n## One-line take\nEnterprise-software founder and operator named among Alation's early individual backers. [src](https://www.parallelwireless.com/company/who-we-are/) [src](https://www.alation.com/news-and-press/alation-raises-9m-in-series-a-funding-led-by-costanoa-venture-capital-dcvc/)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium: Alation supplies early enterprise-data exposure, but the checked sources do not establish a frontier-lab or chip investment mandate. [src](https://www.alation.com/news-and-press/alation-raises-9m-in-series-a-funding-led-by-costanoa-venture-capital-dcvc/)\n\n## Background\nFounded Parallel Wireless in 2012 and was CEO in the 2021 Desktop Metal proxy; the current company page also identifies him as founder and CEO. [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1754820/000110465921082262/tm2119562-1_def14a.htm) [src](https://www.parallelwireless.com/company/who-we-are/)\n\nPreviously founded and led Endeca; also worked on the early Akamai team, led a team at Inktomi, and worked at AT&T Teradata. The company biography does not date those positions. [src](https://www.parallelwireless.com/company/who-we-are/)\n\nHolds a BS from Princeton and MBA from Harvard Business School; graduation dates not stated. Desktop Metal reported his board service beginning in December 2020; current board tenure was not checked. [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1754820/000110465921082262/tm2119562-1_def14a.htm)\n\nPhoto: direct image retrieved with HTTP 200 and image content type on 2026-11-12; originating biography [src](https://www.parallelwireless.com/company/who-we-are/).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [link](https://www.parallelwireless.com/company/who-we-are/) |  |\n| Substack / blog |  | Not confirmed in checked sources. |\n| X |  | Not confirmed in checked sources. |\n| LinkedIn |  | Not confirmed in checked sources. |\n| GitHub |  | Not confirmed in checked sources. |\n| Podcasts (host) |  | Not confirmed in checked sources. |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\n- Date not confirmed — Closing Partner Keynote: Steve Papa — [catalogue source](https://www.fierce-network.com/person/closing-partner-keynote-steve-papa). Not mined.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Read these first\n- [Closing Partner Keynote: Steve Papa](https://www.fierce-network.com/person/closing-partner-keynote-steve-papa) — source to review; no insight summary written.\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed; no follower counts or inferred interaction relationships recorded.\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Alation | Not confirmed | By 2015-03-04 | Not confirmed | Not attributed to a specific round | [src](https://www.alation.com/news-and-press/alation-raises-9m-in-series-a-funding-led-by-costanoa-venture-capital-dcvc/) |\n\nNamed as an individual backer in the Series A announcement. Exact investment round is not confirmed, so no Alation deal row is emitted; only an investor relationship is recorded. [src](https://www.alation.com/news-and-press/alation-raises-9m-in-series-a-funding-led-by-costanoa-venture-capital-dcvc/)\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page)\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Parallel Wireless | at: Founder and CEO; Current role corroborated by company site. | 2012 | [src](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1754820/000110465921082262/tm2119562-1_def14a.htm) |\n| Endeca | at: Former founder and CEO; Historical employment; exact dates not disclosed. | Not confirmed | [src](https://www.parallelwireless.com/company/who-we-are/) |\n| Akamai | at: Former early team member; Historical employment; exact dates not disclosed. | Not confirmed | [src](https://www.parallelwireless.com/company/who-we-are/) |\n| Inktomi | at: Former team leader; Historical employment; exact dates not disclosed. | Not confirmed | [src](https://www.parallelwireless.com/company/who-we-are/) |\n| AT&T Teradata | at: Former employee; Historical employer name as stated in company bio; dates not disclosed. | Not confirmed | [src](https://www.parallelwireless.com/company/who-we-are/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Alation confirms an investor relationship but does not isolate the date or vehicle of Papa's own check. No round-specific deal row is emitted because the announcement does not establish new capital from Papa in that round. [src](https://www.alation.com/news-and-press/alation-raises-9m-in-series-a-funding-led-by-costanoa-venture-capital-dcvc/)\n- Earlier employment and education dates, current location and personal check range remain unconfirmed. [src](https://www.parallelwireless.com/company/who-we-are/)\n- Personal check size and capacity category were not established. `check_size` and `check_capacity` remain blank; a round total or fund size is not an individual check.\n- Media coverage is a starting catalogue, not an exhaustive career archive. No insight rows were created.\n\n## Sources\n- [Source 1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1754820/000110465921082262/tm2119562-1_def14a.htm)\n- [Source 2](https://www.parallelwireless.com/company/who-we-are/)\n- [Source 3](https://www.alation.com/news-and-press/alation-raises-9m-in-series-a-funding-led-by-costanoa-venture-capital-dcvc/)\n- [Source 4](https://www.fierce-network.com/person/closing-partner-keynote-steve-papa)\n", "person:steve-sloane": "\n# Steve Sloane\n\n## One-line take\nMenlo partner focused on the Inflection Fund's Series B/C companies and AI-powered vertical SaaS. [src](https://menlovc.com/team/steve-sloane/)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nLow: Sloane's stated fund focuses on Series B/C, though he has backed AI application companies including Observe.AI and OpenEvidence. [src](https://menlovc.com/team/steve-sloane/)\n\n## Background\n- Menlo says Sloane joined as an associate in 2015, became principal in 2017, and partner in 2019. [src](https://menlovc.com/team/steve-sloane/)\n- Before Menlo, he worked in growth-stage investing at Insight Venture Partners and founded a YC-backed company. [src](https://menlovc.com/team/steve-sloane/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [Menlo profile](https://menlovc.com/team/steve-sloane/) | Firm profile |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNot catalogued in this skeleton.\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Read these first\nNot prioritized yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot assessed (skeleton page).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OpenEvidence | Series D | 2026 | Unknown |  | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/steve-sloane/) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Menlo Ventures | Partner | 2015 | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/steve-sloane/) |\n| Insight Venture Partners | Former growth-stage investor |  | [src](https://menlovc.com/team/steve-sloane/) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Verify the name and dates of Sloane's YC-backed company and individual lead roles at Menlo. (checked: Menlo biography)\n\n## Sources\n- [Menlo profile](https://menlovc.com/team/steve-sloane/)\n", "person:steve-vassallo": "\n# Steve Vassallo\n\n## One-line take\nGeneral Partner at foundation-capital, with a directly attributed Cerebras financing. [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/reinventing-computing-for-ai-a-decade-with-cerebras)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nMedium — the role and/or named company exposure provides a relevant connection to frontier AI, compute, or AI-native software, but this skeleton does not assess decision-making. [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/reinventing-computing-for-ai-a-decade-with-cerebras)\n\n## Background\nNot fully mapped in this skeleton pass. Checked: [current role source](https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/steve-vassallo).\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website / firm profile | [source](https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/steve-vassallo) | Current-role source |\n| Substack / blog |  | Not found in this pass |\n| X |  | Not found in this pass |\n| LinkedIn |  | Not added; no durable profile URL verified |\n| GitHub |  | Not found in this pass |\n| Podcasts (host) |  | Not catalogued in this skeleton pass |\n\n## Podcast and talk appearances\nNot found in this skeleton pass (checked: [current role source](https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/steve-vassallo)).\n\n## How they think\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Read these first\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Social engagement\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Portfolio\n| Company | Round | Date | Led? | Co-investors | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cerebras | Series A | 2016-04 | co-led |  | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/reinventing-computing-for-ai-a-decade-with-cerebras) |\n\n## Deal stories\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Relationships\n| Person / firm | Relationship | Since | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| foundation-capital | at: General Partner | 2007 | [src](https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/steve-vassallo) |\n\n## Open questions\n- Full prior employment history and public-media catalogue (checked: [current role source](https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/steve-vassallo)).\n\n## Sources\n- [Current role / profile](https://foundationcapital.com/team-members/steve-vassallo)\n- [Named financing](https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/reinventing-computing-for-ai-a-decade-with-cerebras)\n", "person:steven-lee": "\n# Steven Lee\n\n## One-line take\nFounder of Seven Stars, an AI-focused pre-seed and seed fund, after investing at SV Angel. [src](https://www.sevenstars.vc/)\n\n## Crux\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Differential insight\nNot written yet (skeleton page).\n\n## Fit with my lens\nHigh: Seven Stars says it backs AI companies from inception, including Corgi, Origin Lab and Doctronic. [src](https://www.sevenstars.vc/)\n\n## Background\n- Seven Stars identifies Lee as its founder and a former SV Angel partner, and names Skild AI, Reflection AI and ElevenLabs among companies he backed or advised. [src](https://www.sevenstars.vc/)\n- Fortune reported the launch of Seven Stars’ $40 million first fund in June 2025. [src](https://fortune.com/2025/06/11/term-sheet-next-steven-lee-an-sv-angel-alum-launches-seven-stars-with-40-million-vc-fund-for-seed-and-pre-seed-ai-startups/)\n\n## Where to find them\n| Channel | Link | Notes (followers, cadence, as of date) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Website | [Seven Stars](https://www.sevenstars.vc/) | Fund site |\n| X | [@stevenl](https://x.com/stevenl) | Linked from fund site |